(window.LINGUA_BOOK=window.LINGUA_BOOK||{}).condense=Object.assign(window.LINGUA_BOOK.condense||{},{"part-009-chapter-9-reclaiming":{"title":{"en":"Chapter 9: Reclaiming Indigenous Languages","zh":"第 9 章：重新使用原住民语言"},"summary":{"en":"Canada's settler government pursued deliberate strategies for decades to eliminate the languages of Indigenous peoples. Indigenous people have long worked to preserve their languages by increasing the number of speakers, and since the 2015 Truth and Reconciliation Commission report the federal government has acknowledged these efforts more openly. This chapter presents interviews with Indigenous experts engaged in that work.","zh":"加拿大的定居者政府数十年来一直推行蓄意消除原住民语言的策略。原住民多年来努力提高本族语言的使用者人数以保存语言，而自 2015 年真相与和解委员会报告以来，联邦政府也更公开地承认了这些努力的重要性。本章收录了多位从事语言保存与复兴工作的原住民专家的访谈。"},"points":[{"en":"Settler governments in Canada long used deliberate strategies to eliminate Indigenous languages.","zh":"加拿大的定居者政府长期以蓄意策略消除原住民语言。"},{"en":"Indigenous communities have worked for years to raise the number of people who speak their languages.","zh":"原住民社群多年来致力于增加会说本族语言的人数。"},{"en":"Since the 2015 Truth and Reconciliation Commission report, the federal government acknowledges these efforts more openly.","zh":"自 2015 年真相与和解委员会报告以来，联邦政府更公开地承认这些努力的重要性。"},{"en":"The chapter consists of interviews with Indigenous experts preserving and reclaiming their languages.","zh":"本章由访谈构成，受访者是保存并重振本族语言的原住民专家。"},{"en":"These experts also help other members of their Nations become speakers of the language.","zh":"这些专家还帮助本民族的其他成员成为该语言的使用者。"}],"terms":[{"en":"Indigenous language","zh":"原住民语言","defEn":"A language originally spoken by the first peoples of a region.","defZh":"某一地区最早居民世代使用的语言，相对于殖民者带入的语言。"},{"en":"language preservation","zh":"语言保存","defEn":"Work that aims to keep a language spoken rather than losing it.","defZh":"旨在使某语言继续被人使用、避免消亡的努力与措施。"},{"en":"reclaiming","zh":"语言复兴（重新使用）","defEn":"Bringing a language back into active use by its community.","defZh":"让某语言在社群中重新恢复活力与日常使用的过程。"},{"en":"Truth and Reconciliation Commission","zh":"真相与和解委员会","defEn":"A Canadian body whose 2015 report addressed harms done to Indigenous peoples.","defZh":"加拿大机构，其 2015 年报告处理原住民所受的历史伤害。"},{"en":"settler government","zh":"定居者政府","defEn":"A government of colonizers who settled on Indigenous lands.","defZh":"由迁入原住民土地的殖民者建立的政府。"}],"examples":[],"body":[{"en":"The chapter opens by recalling what Chapter 1 established: Canada's settler government spent many decades carrying out deliberate strategies intended to wipe out the languages spoken by Indigenous peoples. Those policies were not accidental side effects but planned measures.","zh":"本章开篇重申第 1 章的结论：加拿大的定居者政府曾用数十年时间推行蓄意策略，企图抹除原住民所说的各种语言。这些政策并非偶然的副作用，而是有计划的措施。"},{"en":"Against that history, Indigenous people have worked for years to keep their languages alive, chiefly by increasing the number of people who can speak them. Since the Truth and Reconciliation Commission issued its report in 2015, the federal government has more openly admitted that these efforts matter.","zh":"面对这样的历史，原住民多年来努力使本族语言存续，主要办法是增加会说这些语言的人数。自真相与和解委员会于 2015 年发布报告以来，联邦政府更加公开地承认这些努力的重要性。"},{"en":"The chapter itself is built from interviews with several Indigenous experts. They are the people doing the work of preserving and reclaiming their languages, and also the work of helping other members of their Nations become speakers.","zh":"本章的内容来自对多位原住民专家的访谈。正是这些人从事保存与重振本族语言的工作，也帮助本民族的其他成员学会并开口使用这些语言。"}]},"chapter-105-preserving":{"title":{"en":"9.1 Preserving Mohawk","zh":"9.1 保存莫霍克语"},"summary":{"en":"This unit is drawn from a conversation with David Kanatawakhon-Maracle, a Turtle Clan Mohawk and professor at Western University. He argues that a language survives only when it is widely spoken in public life, not confined to the home, and that real support means learning to speak the language. He describes bilingualism as ordinary, describes himself as both a speaker and literate, and warns that keeping a language indoors leads to its death.","zh":"本单元摘自与 David Kanatawakhon-Maracle 的对话，他是龟族莫霍克人、西安大略大学教授。他认为语言只有被广泛使用于公共生活而非局限于家庭，才能存活；真正的支持是去学习并会说这门语言。他把双语视为常态，说自己既能说也具备读写能力，并警告把语言关在家中将导致其消亡。"},"points":[{"en":"Verbal support means little; genuine support requires enrolling in classes and learning to speak the language.","zh":"口头上的支持意义不大；真正的支持是去上课并学会说这门语言。"},{"en":"He does not expect monolingualism; bilingualism is a normal way of life for much of the world.","zh":"他并不期待单语状态；对世界大部分人口来说，双语是相当正常的生活方式。"},{"en":"His grandparents and great-grandparents were bilingual, using English or Mohawk as needed.","zh":"他的祖父母与曾祖父母都是双语者，视需要分别使用英语或莫霍克语。"},{"en":"As well as speaking Mohawk he reads and writes it, a literacy universities should teach.","zh":"他不仅会说莫霍克语，还能读会写，这种读写能力正是大学应当教授的。"},{"en":"The language must be in the street and the stores, not only the home, or it dies.","zh":"语言必须出现在街头与商店，而不只是家里，否则就会消亡。"},{"en":"Confined to the home, speakers meet only English outside, so the next generation is raised by English speakers.","zh":"语言若被困在家中，使用者在外面只说英语，下一代便由英语使用者抚养长大。"}],"terms":[{"en":"bilingualism","zh":"双语现象","defEn":"The routine use of two languages by a person or community.","defZh":"个人或社群日常使用两种语言的状态。"},{"en":"monolingualism","zh":"单语状态","defEn":"The condition of using only one language.","defZh":"只使用一种语言的状态。"},{"en":"literate","zh":"具备读写能力的","defEn":"Able to read and write a particular language.","defZh":"能用某种语言进行阅读和书写。"},{"en":"home domain","zh":"家庭域","defEn":"The private setting of the household, contrasted with public settings.","defZh":"家庭这一私人场合，与街头、商店等公共场合相对。"},{"en":"language shift","zh":"语言转用","defEn":"A community's gradual replacement of one language by another.","defZh":"某社群逐渐用另一种语言取代原有语言的过程。"}],"examples":[{"text":"my grandfather was bilingual, you know, and my great-grandparents were bilingual and they could use English when they needed to and they used Mohawk when they needed to","noteEn":"Kanatawakhon-Maracle presents bilingualism as the ordinary pattern of earlier generations.","noteZh":"他以此说明双语是上一代人再平常不过的生活状态。"},{"text":"it’s in the street; it’s in the stores; it’s outside of the home","noteEn":"The concrete public settings he says a living language must occupy.","noteZh":"他举出的语言保持活力所必须出现的具体公共场合。"}],"body":[{"en":"This unit comes from a conversation with David Kanatawakhon-Maracle, a Turtle Clan Mohawk who teaches at Western University. His theme is what a language needs in order to survive, and his answer is that it must be widely spoken, not merely admired.","zh":"本单元摘自与 David Kanatawakhon-Maracle 的对话，他是龟族莫霍克人，任教于西安大略大学。他谈的主题是一门语言要存活下去究竟需要什么，而他的答案是：必须被广泛使用，而不只是被称赞。"},{"en":"He has little patience for people whose support is only verbal: they praise the work but never enter his classes and never learn the language. Real support for an Aboriginal language means getting out there, learning it and learning to speak it, bringing it back into its own. He explicitly rejects sole monolingualism as a goal, since bilingualism is a fairly normal way to be for a large percentage of the world's population, and his grandparents and great-grandparents lived that way, using English when they needed to and Mohawk when they needed to.","zh":"他对只停留在口头上的支持者缺乏耐心：他们称赞这项工作，却从不走进他的课堂，也从不学这门语言。对原住民语言真正的支持是走出去、学习它、学会说它，让这门语言重新属于自己。他明确不把完全单语当作目标，因为对世界大部分人口而言，双语是相当正常的生活方式；他的祖父母和曾祖父母就是这样生活，需要时用英语，需要时用莫霍克语。"},{"en":"His own competence is unusual in one respect: he speaks Mohawk and English, but he also reads and writes Mohawk. That literacy is exactly what universities should be teaching, because a great deal of material written in Aboriginal languages is currently unavailable in English, and speakers may not need it in English anyway.","zh":"他本人的语言能力有一点与众不同：他会说莫霍克语和英语，同时还能读写莫霍克语。这种读写能力正是大学应该教授的内容，因为大量以原住民语言写成的材料目前没有英语版本，而对这些语言的使用者来说，也未必需要英语版本。"},{"en":"People often say the language belongs in the home, but he rejects that: the language should be in the street, in the stores, outside the home. Confine it to the home and it dies, because speakers eventually leave that home, meet only English outside, and the next generation is raised by two English speakers. The problem is worse in contemporary society, where people spend more and more time at home in front of technical devices; formerly people moved from home to home, everyone spoke the language, and it stayed very much alive. Once it is ensconced indoors, people can talk to their parents but cannot understand their neighbours.","zh":"人们常说语言属于家庭，但他否定这一点：语言应该在街头、在商店、在家庭之外。把它困在家里，它就会消亡，因为使用者终究会离开家，在外面遇到的只有英语，下一代便由两个说英语的人抚养。在当代社会这个问题更严重，人们越来越多地待在家里面对各种技术设备；从前人们走家串户，人人都在说这门语言，它因此充满活力。一旦它被安置在室内，人们能和父母交谈，却听不懂邻居的话。"}]},"chapter-106-learning-mohawk":{"title":{"en":"9.2 Learning Mohawk","zh":"9.2 学习莫霍克语"},"summary":{"en":"Some Mohawk people acquire Mohawk while growing up among fluent speakers, while many others try to learn it at school or university, where linguistic knowledge can make second-language learning easier. In this unit David Kanatawakhon-Maracle explains that he learned by hearing hundreds of speakers and by spending long hours with older people, who hold tradition and stories. He also describes the value of grammar in his teaching and how linguistics helps students with pronunciation.","zh":"有些莫霍克人在流利使用者身边长大而习得莫霍克语，更多人则在学校或大学里尝试学习它，而语言学知识在某些方面能让第二语言学习更容易。在本单元中，David Kanatawakhon-Maracle 说明自己是靠听数百人说话、并长时间陪伴掌握传统与故事的老人而学会该语言；他也谈到语法教学的价值，以及语言学课程如何帮助学生掌握发音。"},"points":[{"en":"He understands and speaks Mohawk because he has heard hundreds of people speaking it.","zh":"他之所以能听懂并会说莫霍克语，是因为听过数百人讲这门语言。"},{"en":"As a teenager he spent much more time with older people, who were more inclined to be speakers.","zh":"少年时他花更多时间与长辈相处，因为长者更愿意说这门语言。"},{"en":"Older people are guarded at first; repeated visits earn access to what they know and to their stories.","zh":"长者起初戒备，需多次探访才能获得他们的知识与故事的信任通行证。"},{"en":"Elders carry tradition and responsibility to pass it on; if nobody listens, it dies with them.","zh":"长者承载传统与传承责任；若无人愿听，这些内容会随他们一同消逝。"},{"en":"He finds people more amusing in Mohawk, while English makes them cranky and unhappy.","zh":"他觉得人们说莫霍克语时更有趣，说英语时则暴躁不快。"},{"en":"His course is heavily grammar-based; vocabulary without grammar cannot be put to use.","zh":"他的课程以语法为重；没有语法，词汇就无法真正使用。"}],"terms":[{"en":"immersion program","zh":"沉浸式课程","defEn":"A program in which learners are taught through the target language.","defZh":"以目标语言作为教学媒介、使学习者沉浸其中的教学项目。"},{"en":"allophone","zh":"音位变体","defEn":"A predictable variant of a phoneme in a particular environment.","defZh":"同一音位在特定语音环境中出现的可预测变体。"},{"en":"voiced","zh":"浊音的","defEn":"Produced with vibration of the vocal folds.","defZh":"发音时声带振动的语音特征。"},{"en":"voiceless","zh":"清音的","defEn":"Produced without vibration of the vocal folds.","defZh":"发音时声带不振动的语音特征。"},{"en":"Introduction to Linguistics","zh":"语言学导论","defEn":"An introductory course teaching basic concepts such as phonemes and allophones.","defZh":"讲授音位、音位变体等基本概念的一门入门课程。"},{"en":"grammar-based course","zh":"以语法为基础的课程","defEn":"A language course organized chiefly around grammatical structure.","defZh":"主要围绕语法结构组织教学的语言课程。"}],"examples":[{"text":"I understand Mohawk and I speak Mohawk because I’ve heard hundreds of people speaking it.","noteEn":"His own account of how he acquired the language in the community.","noteZh":"他对自己如何在社群中习得该语言的说明。"},{"text":"They get cranky and grumpy and unhappy when they speak English","noteEn":"His description of the same relatives in each language.","noteZh":"他对同一批亲属使用两种语言时状态的描述。"},{"text":"I says yeah, it’s pronounced [d].","noteEn":"His reply to students who cannot get past the written letter t.","noteZh":"他对无法摆脱字母书写形式的学生所作的回应。"},{"text":"this is a, this is an allophone and it’s voiced in these circumstances and voiceless here","noteEn":"The interviewer notes how linguistics coursework explains such pronunciation patterns.","noteZh":"采访者指出语言学课程能解释这类发音规律。"}],"body":[{"en":"Some Mohawk people learn the language by growing up among fluent speakers, and others attempt it in school or at university. The unit notes that knowing some linguistics can, in some ways, make learning a second language easier.","zh":"有些莫霍克人因自幼生活在流利使用者中间而学会这门语言，另一些人则在学校或大学里尝试学习。本单元指出，掌握一些语言学知识在某些方面能让第二语言学习变得更容易。"},{"en":"The speaker explains that he understands and speaks Mohawk because he has heard hundreds of people speaking it. He grew up with Mohawk and English, and as a teenager spent much more time with older people, who were more inclined to be speakers and whom he found more entertaining than younger adults. They were not very trusting, so repeated visits were needed to crack that shell and gain access to what they knew; their responsibility is to pass on tradition, and if they conclude that nobody wants to hear it, it dies with them.","zh":"说话人解释说，他能听懂并会说莫霍克语，是因为听过数百人讲这门语言。他在莫霍克语和英语环境中长大，少年时更多与长辈相处，因为长者更愿意说这门语言，他也觉得长者比年轻成年人更有趣。长者并不轻信他人，需要多次探访才能打破外壳、接触他们所知的内容；他们的责任是传承传统，而如果他们认定无人愿意听，这些内容便会随他们一同逝去。"},{"en":"He also noticed that people were more fun in Mohawk than in English, becoming cranky, grumpy and unhappy when they spoke English. An uncle who complained about everything was grating in English, yet when he spoke Mohawk the family spent far more time laughing, since that was the language of his childhood and his stories from that time were more amusing and interesting than what he faced in the present.","zh":"他还注意到，人们说莫霍克语时比说英语时更有趣，一说英语便暴躁、牢骚满腹、不快。他的一位叔叔对一切都抱怨，用英语时听起来格外刺耳；可当他说莫霍克语时，大家笑的时间多得多，因为那是他童年的语言，来自那段时光的故事也比他在当下面对的事情更有趣、更引人入胜。"},{"en":"As a teacher he receives students who have spent years in the immersion program at Six Nations: they have a sense of the language and a large vocabulary but are not speakers and cannot do anything with that vocabulary. His course is therefore fairly heavily grammar-based, though familiarity with pronunciation is a good starting point. Anglophones, he finds, cannot get past what they see written, objecting that a written t should be pronounced as such; he replies that it is pronounced [d]. The interviewer observes that an Introduction to Linguistics would explain this: it is an allophone, voiced in some circumstances and voiceless in others.","zh":"作为教师，他遇到的学生在六族保留地的沉浸式课程中学习了数年：他们对这门语言有感觉、词汇量很大，却并非使用者，也无法用这些词汇做成任何事情。因此他的课程以语法为相当重的重点，不过熟悉发音是个不错的起点。他发现，英语母语者无法摆脱所看到的书写形式，会反对说写出来的 t 就该那样读；他回答说它读作 [d]。采访者指出，语言学导论会解释这一点：这是一个音位变体，在某些环境中为浊音，在另一些环境中为清音。"}]},"chapter-107-mohawk-culture":{"title":{"en":"Mohawk culture and language","zh":"9.3 莫霍克文化与语言"},"summary":{"en":"Dr. Kanatawakhon-Maracle describes cultural knowledge embedded in Mohawk grammar, including the restricted use of nyaweh, the avoidance of negating the future, and a free word order held together by pronominal prefixes. He also shows that living languages change, as when oháhsera extended from candles to electric lights.","zh":"本单元中，Kanatawakhon-Maracle 博士讲述莫霍克语语法中蕴含的文化要素：nyaweh 的用法远比英语 thank you 严格，使用者不直接否定将来，语序自由而靠代词性前缀连接。他也说明活语言总在变化，例如 oháhsera 从蜡烛扩展到各种人造光源。"},"points":[{"en":"Mohawk nyaweh is not a casual thank-you but an expression directed to the Creator.","zh":"莫霍克语 nyaweh 不是随口道谢，而是面向造物主表达感激的说法。"},{"en":"Fluent speakers avoid negating the future directly, using non-definite forms instead.","zh":"流利使用者不直接否定将来，而是改用非确定形式绕开这种说法。"},{"en":"Mohawk word order is free, held together by pronominal prefixes and shaped by emphasis.","zh":"莫霍克语语序自由，由代词性前缀联结成句，并随强调重点而调整。"},{"en":"A word's accent shifts according to where the word occurs in the sentence.","zh":"一个词的重音会随它在句中出现的位置而转移到别的音节上。"},{"en":"Vocabulary follows culture, as oháhsera moved from candles to modern artificial lighting.","zh":"词汇随文化演变，oháhsera 就从蜡烛一路扩展到现在的人造光源。"}],"terms":[{"en":"nyaweh","zh":"nyaweh（莫霍克语致谢语）","defEn":"Mohawk expression directed to the Creator, loosely translated as thank you.","defZh":"莫霍克语中面向造物主表达感激的说法，英语常译为 thank you，但用法严格得多。"},{"en":"pronominal prefix","zh":"代词性前缀","defEn":"Verb prefix marking the person and number of the subject or object.","defZh":"附着在动词上、标示主语或宾语的人称与数的前缀。"},{"en":"accent shift","zh":"重音转移","defEn":"Movement of a word's stress to another syllable depending on its position.","defZh":"词的重音随该词在句中出现的位置而移到另一个音节上。"},{"en":"semantic drift","zh":"语义漂移","defEn":"Gradual change in a word's meaning as its range of use extends.","defZh":"词义随使用范围扩大而逐渐改变，如 oháhsera 从蜡烛扩展到各种灯具。"},{"en":"negating the future","zh":"否定将来","defEn":"Directly denying a future event, which fluent Mohawk speakers avoid.","defZh":"直接否定将来发生的事，莫霍克语的流利使用者会避免这种说法。"}],"examples":[{"text":"nyaweh","noteEn":"Usually rendered as thank you, but traditionally reserved for the Creator.","noteZh":"通常被译为 thank you，但按旧传统只用于对造物主表达感激。"},{"text":"raksá:’a ire ohaháke","noteEn":"One arrangement of the three words, placing emphasis on the boy.","noteZh":"三个词的一种排列，把强调重点放在「男孩」上。"},{"text":"ohaháke ire raksá:’a","noteEn":"Another arrangement of the same words, emphasizing the road.","noteZh":"同样三个词的另一种排列，把强调重点放在「路」上。"},{"text":"kahiatonhseráke","noteEn":"On the book, with the accent shifted to the penultimate syllable.","noteZh":"意为「在书上」，重音已转移到倒数第二个音节。"}],"body":[{"en":"Dr. Kanatawakhon-Maracle teaches culture together with the language, explaining where a word or expression comes from. Mohawk nyaweh is generally interpreted as thank you in English, but in the older tradition he was raised in it is not said for every small thing. It is directed to the Creator, for a sunset, a flower or majestic scenery, and at a shared meal the first person to rise says it for the opportunity to sit with others. Younger speakers now use it much as English does.","zh":"Kanatawakhon-Maracle 博士把文化与语言放在一起教授，讲明一个词或表达法从哪里来。莫霍克语的 nyaweh 一般被英语解释为 thank you，但在他所遵循的旧传统里，它并不用于每一件小事：它指向造物主，用于日落、花朵或壮丽的景色；一同用餐时，最先起身的人说 nyaweh，感谢的是能与他人共坐的机会。年轻的使用者现在已像英语那样随意使用它。"},{"en":"Some things, he argues, should not be updated, because change erodes a language's uniqueness: if you speak Mohawk the way you speak English, why not simply speak English? English can say it will not snow today, but Mohawk speakers do not negate the future directly. A negative future can be constructed, yet nobody uses it; speakers turn instead to a non-definite form meaning something like it would not or there is a possibility that it will not.","zh":"他认为有些东西不该更新，因为改动会侵蚀一种语言的独特性：如果像说英语那样说莫霍克语，那为什么不干脆说英语？英语可以说 it will not snow today，但莫霍克语使用者不会直接否定将来。否定式的将来其实可以构造，却没有人使用；使用者改用非确定形式，意思近于「它不会吧」或「有可能它不会」。"},{"en":"Mohawk word order, unlike the fixed English subject-verb-object, is quite literally whatever comes out of your mouth, tied together by pronominal prefixes. A word's accent moves with its position: kahiatónhsera, book, accents tón, while kahiatonhseráke, on the book, shifts the accent to the penultimate syllable. Position also depends on emphasis and on answering a question, since the information asked for comes first, yielding six arrangements of three words.","zh":"与英语固定的主-动-宾语序不同，莫霍克语语序简直就是说出口的顺序，靠代词性前缀串联成句。词的重音随位置移动：kahiatónhsera（书）重音在 tón，kahiatonhseráke（在书上）则把重音移到倒数第二音节。词的位置还取决于强调和是否在回答问题，被问及的信息会放在最前，于是三个词可有六种排列。"},{"en":"Vocabulary likewise follows culture: stop using a stone axe and its word will eventually disappear. Other words are carried forward, like oháhsera, a light. It first named something shaped much like a bone, oháhsa with the -ra suffix; candles resembled that bone, so candles were called oháhsera, then lamps of similar shape, then ceiling and wall lights. The word lasted because its basic application remained while the shape kept changing.","zh":"词汇也随文化而变：不再使用石斧，石斧的词终会消失。另一些词则沿用下来，比如 oháhsera（光）。它原本指很像一块骨头的东西，即 oháhsa 加后缀 -ra；因为蜡烛像那块骨头，蜡烛就叫 oháhsera，接着是形状相近的灯，后来连顶灯和壁灯也算。这个词延续至今，是因为基本用途还在，只是形状不断变化。"}]},"chapter-108-creating-materials":{"title":{"en":"Creating materials for teaching Mohawk","zh":"9.4 为莫霍克语教学编写教材"},"summary":{"en":"Dr. Kanatawakhon-Maracle explains how he built his own Mohawk teaching materials when almost nothing was available, and how Günther Michelson's A Thousand Words of Mohawk convinced him to organize his courses around roots. He describes learning to ask the right question, and why working with fluent speakers requires grammatical and metalinguistic awareness.","zh":"Kanatawakhon-Maracle 博士讲述在几乎没有任何现成材料的情况下，如何自己编写莫霍克语教材，以及 Günther Michelson 的《A Thousand Words of Mohawk》如何使他决定围绕词根组织教学。他还谈到如何学会提出正确的问题，以及为什么与流利使用者合作需要语法意识和元语言意识。"},"points":[{"en":"Almost no Mohawk teaching materials existed, so he had to buy supplies and write his own.","zh":"当时几乎没有现成的莫霍克语教学材料，他只能自购教具并自己编写。"},{"en":"Michelson's root-based dictionary showed him a better way to organize a language course.","zh":"Michelson 按词根编排的词典让他看到组织语言课程的一种更好方式。"},{"en":"Linguistic writing was unreadable to him unless it supplied plenty of worked examples.","zh":"语言学著作若没有大量可用的实例，他就完全读不懂。"},{"en":"He learned by trial and error to ask the right question when working with speakers.","zh":"他在与使用者合作中反复摸索，才学会怎样提出正确的问题。"},{"en":"Fluent speakers may lack metalinguistic awareness and mix pronominal categories in their answers.","zh":"流利使用者可能缺乏元语言意识，回答时会混用不同的代词性范畴。"}],"terms":[{"en":"root","zh":"词根","defEn":"Basic morpheme from which related words are formed, the basis of Michelson's dictionary.","defZh":"构成相关词的基础语素，也是 Michelson 词典编排所依据的单位。"},{"en":"metalinguistic awareness","zh":"元语言意识","defEn":"Ability to reflect consciously and explicitly on how one's own language works.","defZh":"有意识地、明确地反思自己语言如何运作的能力。"},{"en":"fluent speaker","zh":"流利使用者","defEn":"Someone who uses the language easily but may lack explicit grammatical knowledge.","defZh":"能自如使用该语言、却未必具备明确语法知识的人。"},{"en":"elicitation","zh":"语料诱导","defEn":"Collecting language data by asking speakers carefully framed questions.","defZh":"通过向使用者提出精心设计的问题来收集语言材料。"},{"en":"pronominal categories","zh":"代词性范畴","defEn":"Subjective, objective and transitive sets of pronominal markers on the verb.","defZh":"动词上区分主语、宾语和及物的一组组代词性标记。"}],"examples":[{"text":"kerhitáke","noteEn":"Offered by a speaker for tree, but actually meaning on the tree.","noteZh":"使用者给出的「树」的说法，其实意思是「在树上」。"},{"text":"tkerhitoke","noteEn":"There is a tree standing there, one of several related forms he collected.","noteZh":"意为「那里立着一棵树」，是他收集到的若干相关形式之一。"},{"text":"kérhite","noteEn":"The form he eventually identified as the word for tree.","noteZh":"他最终确认的表示「树」的那个词。"},{"text":"wahahní:no","noteEn":"He just bought it, contrasted with rohahní:no, he bought it a while ago.","noteZh":"意为「他刚买了」，与表示「他很久以前买了」的 rohahní:no 相对。"}],"body":[{"en":"Dr. Kanatawakhon-Maracle has taught Mohawk since about 1990-91, first taking the course here partly to test whether a textbook he had developed would work. He has since written five language-learning textbooks with exercises and drills, plus a companion volume of Supplements divided into ten areas covering numbers, locations and the like. When the band council offered Mohawk in the public school back home, he returned to teach it, being quite literally the youngest speaker there, driving to four schools daily for half an hour of language per class from Kindergarten to Grade 8.","zh":"Kanatawakhon-Maracle 博士自 1990 至 1991 年前后开始教莫霍克语，最初在这里开课，部分是想检验自己编写的教材是否可行。后来他写了五套语言学习教材，配有练习和操练，另有一本 Supplements 配套书，分为十个部分，涉及数字、方位等。家乡的部族议会开设莫霍克语课后，他回去任教，在那里几乎就是最年轻的使用者，每天开车跑四所学校，每班半小时语言课，从幼儿园一直教到八年级。"},{"en":"There were no materials whatsoever and the pay was a hundred dollars a week, so he bought his own flashcard supplies and developed his own material. In his fourth year he came across Günther Michelson's A Thousand Words of Mohawk, organized around roots, and it made so much sense that he began to think this was the better way to teach. Much linguistic work on Iroquoian languages was then largely unreadable to him; he needed sources with enough examples to work out what was being claimed.","zh":"当时什么材料都没有，报酬是每周一百元，他只好自己买做闪示卡的用具并自编材料。教书第四年，他遇到 Günther Michelson 的《A Thousand Words of Mohawk》，这本书按词根编排，让他觉得很有道理，于是开始考虑这或许是更好的教学路子。当时关于易洛魁语系语言的许多语言学著作对他几乎读不懂，他需要实例充足的文献才能弄明白对方在说什么。"},{"en":"Working with speakers, he learned by trial and error to ask the right question, since speakers tell you whatever comes to mind. Asked for the word for tree, a speaker offers kerhitáke, which turns out to mean on the tree; later he learned kerhitákon was in the tree, and forms like tkerhitoke eventually led him to kérhite. With I trust him the problem recurs: trust him for money, for what he says, or to get the job done? Fluent speakers such as an eighty-five-year-old aunt may have no sense of the grammar and mix the subjective, objective and transitive pronominals, saying wahahní:no one day and rohahní:no the next for he bought, which differ in how recent the buying was.","zh":"与使用者合作时，他靠反复试错学会提出正确的问题，因为使用者想到什么就说什么。问「树」怎么说，对方给出 kerhitáke，其实是「在树上」；后来他知道 kerhitákon 是「在树里」，而 tkerhitoke 这类形式最终让他确定 kérhite 才是「树」。像「我相信他」这样的句子也有同样的困扰：是相信他借钱、相信他说的话，还是相信他能办成事？流利使用者如一位八十五岁的姑母可能对语法毫无概念，会混用主语、宾语和及物这几套代词性标记，昨天说 wahahní:no、今天说 rohahní:no 都表示「他买了」，差别在于购买是否发生在不久前。"}]},"chapter-109-speaking-reconciliation":{"title":{"en":"Speaking Mohawk and reconciliation","zh":"9.5 说莫霍克语与和解"},"summary":{"en":"Dr. Kanatawakhon-Maracle argues that language will play a vital role in reconciliation, and notes that learning a language never ends. He recalls a time when farmers around the territory and shopkeepers in Deseronto spoke Mohawk for trade, and treats that mutual accommodation, and the respect shown by learning someone's language, as an aspect of reconciliation.","zh":"Kanatawakhon-Maracle 博士认为语言将在和解中发挥重要作用，并指出语言学习永无终点。他回忆当年领地周围的农场主和 Deseronto 的店主都会说莫霍克语以便做生意，并把这种彼此迁就、以及愿意学习对方语言所显示的尊重，视为和解的一个方面。"},"points":[{"en":"Whether students pass Mohawk to their children is up to them, and finding a speaking partner is the real test.","zh":"学生是否把莫霍克语传给下一代取决于他们自己，找到同样说这门语言的伴侣才是真正的考验。"},{"en":"Learning a language lasts a lifetime, since even speakers keep meeting unfamiliar vocabulary.","zh":"语言学习是一辈子的事，即使已经是使用者，也仍会不断遇到没听过的词。"},{"en":"Farmers and Deseronto shopkeepers once spoke Mohawk for trade with people from the territory.","zh":"农场主和 Deseronto 的店主曾为了与领地居民做生意而说莫霍克语。"},{"en":"Two groups accommodating each other's language is, to him, an aspect of reconciliation.","zh":"在他看来，两个群体互相迁就对方的语言，本身就是和解的一个方面。"},{"en":"Taking time to learn someone's language shows a certain respect for them.","zh":"愿意花时间学习对方的语言，表明了对对方的一定尊重。"}],"terms":[{"en":"reconciliation","zh":"和解","defEn":"The process of two groups restoring a relationship after past harms.","defZh":"两个群体在经历过去伤害之后重建关系的进程。"},{"en":"lifelong learning","zh":"终身学习","defEn":"The view that acquiring language continues even after one becomes a speaker.","defZh":"认为即使已成为使用者，语言习得仍会持续下去的观点。"},{"en":"language maintenance","zh":"语言保持","defEn":"Efforts to keep a language in use when a dominant language surrounds it.","defZh":"在强势语言环绕之下继续使用本族语言所付出的努力。"},{"en":"mutual accommodation","zh":"相互迁就","defEn":"Each group making the effort to speak in the other's language.","defZh":"两个群体各自努力用对方的语言进行交流。"}],"examples":[{"text":"you could go into stores in Deseronto and shopkeepers would","noteEn":"Shopkeepers there once dealt with people in Mohawk for buying and selling.","noteZh":"那里的店主当年会用莫霍克语和人们打交道，处理买卖之事。"},{"text":"we’ll speak your language because we need your goods","noteEn":"One side of the give and take between two reconciled groups.","noteZh":"和解的两个群体之间彼此互让的一方理由。"},{"text":"when people take time to learn your language, they do have a certain respect","noteEn":"Learning another's language is read as a sign of respect.","noteZh":"学习他人的语言被看作一种尊重的表示。"}],"body":[{"en":"He thinks it is really up to the student whether they will speak Mohawk to their own children. Learning the language may seem difficult, but finding a compatible partner who also speaks it is the real test; some of his students have found such partners and are raising children together in the language. People do not realize that learning a language is a lifelong process. Even as a speaker he still runs across unfamiliar words, or words he has heard but never had time to work out the context for, so a speaker remains always in a state of language learning.","zh":"他认为，学生将来是否对自己的孩子说莫霍克语，完全取决于他们自己。学会这门语言也许已经很难，但找到一位同样说这门语言的合适伴侣才是真正的考验；他的一些学生确实找到了这样的伴侣，正一起用这门语言养育孩子。人们没有意识到，学习语言是一辈子的事。即使已经是使用者，他仍会遇到没听过的词，或者听过却没时间弄清语境的词，所以使用者始终处于语言学习的状态之中。"},{"en":"Language, he says, is something we need; he does not know how we function without it. Reconciliation belongs here too. Years ago in his home community, many farmers living around the territory also spoke Mohawk, at least minimally, because they hired many people from the territory to work on their farms, and in Deseronto shops the shopkeepers would deal with people in Mohawk, a variety tied to buying and selling. To him, that is an aspect of reconciliation: two groups that have come to terms with each other, each with its own interest, since one wants the money and the other needs the goods.","zh":"他说，语言是我们需要的东西，他无法想象没有语言人怎么运转。和解也与此相关。多年前在他的家乡社区，领地周围的许多农场主也会说莫霍克语，哪怕只是最低限度，因为他们雇用了许多领地居民到农场干活；在 Deseronto 的店铺里，店主会用莫霍克语与人们打交道，那是一种与买卖相关的说法。在他看来，这就是和解的一个方面：两个群体彼此达成相处之道，各有自己的利益——一方想要钱，另一方需要货物。"},{"en":"There is always give and take between any two groups that have reconciled, but he also believes that when people take time to learn your language, they do have a certain respect. The ones who want to interact more are the ones who learn more of the language.","zh":"任何两个和解的群体之间总是有来有往，但他同样相信，人们愿意花时间去学你的语言，就说明他们确实怀有一定的尊重；而那些希望有更多交往的人，也会学得更多。"}]},"chapter-110-future":{"title":{"en":"9.6 One view on the future of Indigenous languages","zh":"9.6 关于原住民语言未来的一种观点"},"summary":{"en":"Dr. Kanatawakhon-Maracle argues that Canada should present itself as multilingual rather than bilingual, with Indigenous languages at the table because their speakers were here when English and French arrived. He describes how respect for those languages faded once settlers no longer needed them, and how learning Mohawk means learning Mohawk culture and identity.","zh":"Kanatawakhon-Maracle 博士主张，加拿大应把自己说成多语言国家而非双语国家，原住民语言必须坐到谈判桌前，因为英语和法语到来时，这些语言的使用者早已在此。他讲述了一旦定居者不再需要原住民语言，对其的尊重便消失殆尽，并指出学习莫霍克语就是学习莫霍克文化与身份。"},"points":[{"en":"Canada calls itself bilingual, but including the two founding languages plus Indigenous languages still means about fifty-five languages.","zh":"加拿大自称双语国家，但把两种建国语言与原住民语言都算进来，仍有大约五十五种语言。"},{"en":"Early English and French arrivals did learn Indigenous languages out of necessity, because they needed what Indigenous peoples had.","zh":"早期的英法到来者出于需要确实学过原住民语言，因为他们需要原住民所拥有的东西。"},{"en":"Once settlers wanted the land and pushed Indigenous peoples aside, respect for the languages went out the door.","zh":"一旦定居者觊觎土地并把原住民赶开，对语言的尊重也就随之荡然无存。"},{"en":"Language gives Native people a greater sense of who they are, and learning Mohawk means learning Mohawk culture.","zh":"语言让原住民更清楚自己是谁，而学习莫霍克语的同时也在学习莫霍克文化。"},{"en":"He asks to be known by national names such as Mohawk, Oneida, Ojibwe and Chippewa rather than shifting umbrella labels.","zh":"他要求人们用莫霍克、奥奈达、奥吉布瓦、齐佩瓦这样的民族名称来认识他们，而不是不断更换的统称标签。"}],"terms":[{"en":"Indigenous languages","zh":"原住民语言","defEn":"Languages spoken by the original peoples of a land, before settler colonization.","defZh":"在定居者殖民之前，由某片土地上原有民族使用的语言。"},{"en":"multilingual country","zh":"多语言国家","defEn":"A nation whose population regularly uses more than two languages.","defZh":"指全国人口日常使用两种以上语言、多语并存的国家情形。"},{"en":"colonialism","zh":"殖民主义","defEn":"Settlement and control of a territory and its people by an outside power.","defZh":"外来强权对某片土地及其人民实行定居占领与控制的制度。"},{"en":"language and identity","zh":"语言与身份","defEn":"The link between speaking a language and knowing who one is.","defZh":"使用某种语言与确认自身族群身份、文化归属之间的紧密联系。"}],"examples":[{"text":"when I was born I was born an Indian but then I became a Native and then I became Aboriginal and then I became First Nations and now I’m Indigenous, yay!","noteEn":"He lists the succession of official labels applied to him across his lifetime.","noteZh":"他列举了自己一生中被依次套上的官方标签，用反讽语气呈现这种更名。"},{"text":"Mohawk, Oneida, Ojibwe, Chippewa","noteEn":"The national names he would rather be known by than umbrella terms.","noteZh":"他希望人们用来称呼他们的民族名称，而非笼统的统称。"},{"text":"If I focus on speaking Mohawk then in the process of learning my language I’m also learning Mohawk culture","noteEn":"Learning the language is inseparable from learning the culture that goes with it.","noteZh":"学习语言与学习文化不可分割，二者在同一过程中完成。"}],"body":[{"en":"Dr. Kanatawakhon-Maracle argues that Canada, which presents itself as a bilingual country, would do better to describe itself as multilingual. Even if multilingualism were defined as the two founding languages plus the Indigenous languages, he points out, that still amounts to roughly fifty-five languages, and Indigenous languages have to be at the table because their speakers were already here when English and French showed up and built the present-day institutions. Everyone else who comes has, in effect, read the brochure and knows which languages to learn.","zh":"Kanatawakhon-Maracle 博士认为，自称双语国家的加拿大不如把自己说成多语言国家。他指出，即便把多语言状态界定为两种建国语言加上原住民语言，那也仍然有大约五十五种语言；而且原住民语言必须坐到桌前，因为英语和法语出现并建立当今各种制度时，这些语言的使用者早已在此。其他后来者实际上都读过那份介绍材料，知道自己该学哪种语言。"},{"en":"He notes that the newcomers did initially learn Indigenous languages in order to deal with their speakers, since they had little choice: Indigenous peoples held what the settlers wanted. When that stopped being true and the land itself became the object, Indigenous peoples were pushed aside and respect for the languages disappeared, after which the assumption became that everyone here speaks English only.","zh":"他指出，新来者最初确实学过原住民语言以便与使用者打交道，因为他们别无选择：原住民掌握着定居者想要的东西。而当这一点不再成立、土地本身成为目标时，原住民被推开，对语言的尊重也随之消失，此后便形成了这里人人都只说英语的预设。"},{"en":"For him the language is a source of identity. Years under the colonial thumb convinced many Native people that their languages and traditions were inferior, but no amount of effort makes a Native person into a non-Native Canadian; focusing on Mohawk means learning Mohawk culture and what it is to be Mohawk. He also resists the sequence of labels — Indian, Native, Aboriginal, First Nations, Indigenous — and asks instead to be known by distinct nationalities and cultures: Mohawk, Oneida, Ojibwe, Chippewa.","zh":"对他而言，语言是身份的来源。多年处于殖民统治之下，让许多原住民相信自己的语言和传统低人一等，但无论怎样努力，原住民也不会因此变成非原住民的加拿大人；专注于莫霍克语，就是在学习莫霍克文化以及身为莫霍克人的意义。他也不认同那一连串标签——Indian、Native、Aboriginal、First Nations、Indigenous——而希望人们通过彼此不同的民族与文化来认识他们：莫霍克、奥奈达、奥吉布瓦、齐佩瓦。"}]},"chapter-111-slug-9-7-reclaiming-michif":{"title":{"en":"9.7 Reclaiming Michif","zh":"9.7 重拾米奇夫语"},"summary":{"en":"Chantale Cenerini, a citizen of the Métis Nation Saskatchewan, discusses the multilingual history of the Michif/Métis people, whose large homeland means no single language is shared across the nation. She describes documenting community stories, supporting adult classes and developing an interactive verb game, and the challenges adults face because Michif verbs come from Cree with a complex Algonquian structure.","zh":"梅蒂斯民族萨斯喀彻温公民 Chantale Cenerini 讲述了米奇夫／梅蒂斯人多语言的历史：其家园幅员辽阔，整个民族并不共用一种语言。她介绍了记录社区故事、支持成人课堂和开发互动动词游戏的工作，以及成人学习者因米奇夫语动词源自克里语、具有复杂的阿尔冈昆结构而面临的困难。"},"points":[{"en":"As a post-contact Indigenous people, the Michif/Métis have always been multilingual, dealing with European traders and with Cree, Ojibwe and Saulteaux relatives.","zh":"梅蒂斯人与米奇夫人是接触后形成的原住民族群，历来多语并用，与欧洲商人及克里、奥吉布瓦等亲戚往来。"},{"en":"The Michif homeland spreads across the western provinces and into North Dakota, so communities do not all share one language.","zh":"米奇夫家园横跨西部各省并延伸至北达科他，因此各社区并不共用同一种语言。"},{"en":"Cenerini documents stories told in the community language, which banks the language, gives it prestige and can preserve family history.","zh":"Cenerini 记录以社区语言讲述的故事，这既保存语言、提升其声望，也可能留存家族历史。"},{"en":"Community classes pair Elders who speak the language with teachers, linguists and resource developers who support them.","zh":"社区课堂把会说语言的老人与教师、语言学家和资源开发者配对，共同提供支持。"},{"en":"Adult learners struggle because Michif verbs come from Cree and the language has a complex gender system absent from English.","zh":"成人学习者困难重重，因为米奇夫语动词来自克里语，且有英语所无的复杂性系统。"},{"en":"Communities respond better to linguists who genuinely try to learn and speak the language, becoming learners themselves.","zh":"社区更认可那些真心努力学习和使用该语言、自己成为学习者的语言学家。"}],"terms":[{"en":"language reclamation","zh":"语言复兴","defEn":"Efforts by a community to bring back and take ownership of its language.","defZh":"社区为让本族语言回归、由本族掌握并继续使用而开展的努力。"},{"en":"language documentation","zh":"语言记录","defEn":"Recording and archiving a language through stories, texts and audio.","defZh":"通过故事、文本、录音等方式记录并归档一种语言的系统工作。"},{"en":"mixed language","zh":"混合语","defEn":"A language combining major components, here French nouns and Cree verbs.","defZh":"由不同来源的主要成分组合而成的语言，此处为法语名词加克里语动词。"},{"en":"Algonquian verb structure","zh":"阿尔冈昆语动词结构","defEn":"A complex verb system typical of Algonquian languages such as Cree.","defZh":"克里语等阿尔冈昆语系语言典型的高度复杂的动词系统。"},{"en":"heritage language learner","zh":"传承语学习者","defEn":"Someone learning a family or ancestral language no longer spoken at home.","defZh":"学习家族或祖先语言、但该语言已不在家庭中日常使用的人。"}],"examples":[{"text":"Come in with a good heart.","noteEn":"Advice from John Medicine Horse Kelly at an ethics institute that stayed with Cenerini.","noteZh":"研究伦理讲习班上一位长者的忠告，Cenerini 始终铭记在心。"},{"text":"Round Prairie or La Prairie Ronde","noteEn":"A wintering camp whose Michif descendants now live in Saskatoon.","noteZh":"一个冬季营地，其后代米奇夫家庭如今生活在萨斯卡通。"},{"text":"the verbs come from Cree","noteEn":"The key structural fact that makes Michif verbs unfamiliar to English speakers.","noteZh":"这一关键结构事实使米奇夫语动词对英语母语者十分陌生。"}],"body":[{"en":"Chantale Cenerini is an assistant professor at the University of Saskatchewan and a citizen of the Métis Nation Saskatchewan, researching the languages of the Michif/Métis people, Algonquian languages, and French in Manitoba and Saskatchewan. As a post-contact Indigenous people, the Métis and Michifs have always been multilingual, she says, dealing with European traders and settlers as well as with Cree, Ojibwe and Saulteaux relatives. Their homeland spreads across the western provinces and into North Dakota, so no single language is shared by the whole nation; a common consciousness holds it together, and work must be focused locally, community by community.","zh":"Chantale Cenerini 是萨斯喀彻温大学助理教授，也是梅蒂斯民族萨斯喀彻温的公民，研究米奇夫／梅蒂斯人的语言、阿尔冈昆语系语言以及马尼托巴和萨斯喀彻温的法语。她指出，作为接触后形成的原住民族群，梅蒂斯人和米奇夫人一直多语并用，既与欧洲商人和定居者往来，也与克里、奥吉布瓦、索托的亲戚交流。他们的家园横跨西部各省并延伸至北达科他，整个民族并不共用一种语言，维系他们的是共同的民族意识，因此相关工作必须就地开展，逐社区推进。"},{"en":"Her role varies from community to community. She documents stories told in the community language — accounts of growing up, family and community history — which bank and document the language while raising its prestige. She also facilitates community-driven projects, including adult classes in Saskatoon taught by speakers who are mostly Elders, with teachers and linguists sharing their skills. From this came resource development, notably an interactive verb game where learners manipulate pieces of a verb to build a sentence, making the Algonquian verb less intimidating. Adults who know only English face a complex gender system and an unfamiliar verb structure, since the verbs come from Cree.","zh":"她的角色因社区而异。她记录以社区语言讲述的故事——关于在社区中长大、关于家庭与社区历史的叙述，这些工作既保存和记录语言，也提升其声望。她也为社区主导的项目担任推动者，其中包括萨斯卡通的成人课程：授课者多为老人，教师和语言学家各展所长。由此又发展出资源建设，例如一款互动动词游戏，学习者操作动词的各个成分来造句，让阿尔冈昆语动词不再那么可怕。只会英语的成人要面对复杂的性系统和陌生的动词结构，因为动词来自克里语。"},{"en":"Michif speakers are at least in their seventies and in demand, so willing teachers are increasingly hard to find; Zoom now lets Saskatoon courses reach Métis people in British Columbia and Regina and reconnect relatives. Communities have had mixed relations with academic linguists, though Peter Bakker’s work is still well regarded, and people think better of researchers who genuinely try to learn and speak the language. The advice Cenerini carried away from an ethics institute led by John Medicine Horse Kelly was simple: come in with a good heart.","zh":"米奇夫语的使用者至少已年过七旬且供不应求，愿意授课的人越来越难找；Zoom 让萨斯卡通的课程覆盖到不列颠哥伦比亚和里贾纳的梅蒂斯人，并让亲戚重新联系上。社区与学院派语言学家的关系时好时坏，Peter Bakker 的工作至今仍受敬重，而人们往往更看重那些真心努力学习和使用该语言的研究者。Cenerini 从 John Medicine Horse Kelly 主持的伦理讲习班上带走的一句话很简单：带着善心而来。"}]},"chapter-112-slug-9-8":{"title":{"en":"9.8 Reclaiming Hul’q’umi’num’","zh":"9.8 重拾 Hul’q’umi’num’ 语"},"summary":{"en":"Rae Anne Claxton, from Tsawout and Cowichan, describes reclaiming Hul’q’umi’num’ within her family after the loss of her grandmother. She discusses the emotional difficulty of adult language learning, the fear of speaking rooted in generations of language loss, and her hope that documentation made by earlier linguists will be returned to communities in accessible forms.","zh":"来自 Tsawout 与 Cowichan 的 Rae Anne Claxton 讲述了在祖母去世后于家族内部重拾 Hul’q’umi’num’ 语的经历。她谈到成人语言学习在情感上的艰难、源于数代语言流失的开口恐惧，以及她的期望：早期语言学家所做的记录能以可用的形式回到社区。"},"points":[{"en":"Claxton heard Hul’q’umi’num’ from her grandparents, who were L1 speakers, but was never encouraged to speak back.","zh":"Claxton 的祖父母说 Hul’q’umi’num’ 语，是第一语言使用者，但没鼓励她回应。"},{"en":"After her grandmother, the last fluent speaker in her direct family, died, she began reclaiming the language within her family.","zh":"在直系家庭中最后一位流利使用者祖母去世后，她开始在家族内部重拾这门语言。"},{"en":"Adult learning is emotional and demanding, requiring courage and self-discipline while speakers are being lost and elders are grieved.","zh":"成人学习情感负担重且要求高，在使用者不断离世、又要哀悼老人的同时，需要勇气与自律。"},{"en":"Studying linguistics gave her explanations for phenomena such as the diminutive form her grandmother used for her youngest daughter.","zh":"学习语言学让她能解释祖母对年幼女儿使用指小形式这类现象。"},{"en":"Relations with linguistics have not always been easy, and communities need earlier documentation made accessible to them.","zh":"与语言学的关系并不总是顺畅，社区需要能够获取早期所做的语言记录。"},{"en":"She wants students to know that Indigenous peoples valued linguistic diversity, never forcing others to conform to another language.","zh":"她希望学生知道，原住民珍视语言多样性，从不强迫他人改用别的语言。"}],"terms":[{"en":"Hul’q’umi’num’","zh":"Hul’q’umi’num’ 语","defEn":"The Island dialect of Halkomelem spoken on Vancouver Island.","defZh":"温哥华岛一带使用的哈尔科梅莱姆语岛屿方言，属当地原住民语言。"},{"en":"Halkomelem","zh":"哈尔科梅莱姆语","defEn":"The language whose dialects include Island Halkomelem and two Lower Mainland varieties.","defZh":"一个语言，其方言包括岛屿哈尔科梅莱姆语以及低陆平原的两种变体。"},{"en":"L1 speaker","zh":"第一语言使用者","defEn":"A person who acquired the language as their first language in childhood.","defZh":"在童年时期将该语言作为第一语言自然习得、并以此为主要语言的人。"},{"en":"diminutive","zh":"指小形式","defEn":"A word form marking smallness or endearment, as with a younger child.","defZh":"表示细小、亲昵或年幼的构词形式，如称呼小孩时使用。"},{"en":"Mentor-Apprentice Program","zh":"导师—学徒计划","defEn":"A program pairing a fluent speaker with an adult learner over time.","defZh":"把流利使用者与成人学习者长期配对、共同学习语言的项目。"},{"en":"language nest","zh":"语言巢","defEn":"An immersion setting where young children are surrounded by the language.","defZh":"让幼儿沉浸在目标语言中的沉浸式照护与语言学习环境。"}],"examples":[{"text":"sts’a’muqw","noteEn":"The form her grandmother used for her eldest daughter, later understood as diminutive.","noteZh":"祖母用来称呼她大女儿的形式，她后来才明白这是指小形式。"},{"text":"Huy ch q’u Margaret.","noteEn":"Claxton opens her self-introduction with a Hul’q’umi’num’ greeting of thanks.","noteZh":"Claxton 用 Hul’q’umi’num’ 语的致谢问候开始自我介绍。"},{"text":"when we lose an elder, we lose a library","noteEn":"A saying she cites about the knowledge lost when an Elder dies.","noteZh":"她引用的一句俗语，说明老人去世时随之失去的知识。"},{"text":"you can’t do anything without making mistakes. Making mistakes is how you learn","noteEn":"Her granny’s reassurance, which she applies to her own speaking.","noteZh":"祖母给她的宽慰，她用来鼓励自己开口说话。"}],"body":[{"en":"Rae Anne Claxton was born in Quw’utsun (Cowichan) on Vancouver Island and raised between there and SȾÁUTW̱ (Tsawout First Nation). Her Tsawout grandmother spoke Hul’q’umi’num’, both grandparents in Quw’utsun were L1 speakers, and she learned a little SENĆOŦEN in Saanich. She heard the language and was spoken to in it, but was never encouraged to answer. Hul’q’umi’num’ is the island dialect of Halkomelem, with two further dialects in the Lower Mainland.","zh":"Rae Anne Claxton 出生于温哥华岛的 Quw’utsun（科维昌），在 Quw’utsun 与 SȾÁUTW̱（Tsawout 第一民族）两地长大。她住在 Tsawout 的祖母说 Hul’q’umi’num’ 语，Quw’utsun 的祖父母都是该语言的第一语言使用者，她在萨尼奇期间还学了一点 SENĆOŦEN 语。她听过这门语言，也被用这门语言搭话，却从未被鼓励开口回应。Hul’q’umi’num’ 语是哈尔科梅莱姆语的岛屿方言，低陆平原还通行另外两种方言。"},{"en":"After her grandmother, the last fluent speaker in her direct family, died, Claxton began reclaiming the language within her family. She earned a certificate in Hul’q’umi’num’ proficiency at Simon Fraser University, helped establish a Language Nest in Cowichan, worked with children in care through Cowichan Tribes, and developed books, songs and poems for learners. She is in the third year of the First Peoples Cultural Council’s Mentor-Apprentice Program with her si’lu, grandmother Sarah Modeste.","zh":"在直系家庭中最后一位流利使用者祖母去世后，Claxton 开始在家族内部重拾这门语言。她在西蒙弗雷泽大学取得 Hul’q’umi’num’ 语语言能力证书，参与在科维昌创建语言巢，通过科维昌部落与寄养儿童一起工作，并为学习者编写书籍、歌曲和诗歌。她目前正与自己的 si’lu（祖母）Sarah Modeste 一起，进入第一民族文化遗产委员会导师—学徒计划的第三年。"},{"en":"She calls adult language learning emotional, hard work that takes courage, strength and self-discipline, especially as first-language speakers are lost, elders are grieved, and the pandemic removed spaces for conversation. The fear of speaking, which she links to blood memory and generations of language loss, is what she has had to overcome; her granny told her that mistakes are how you learn. Linguistics helped her explain such phenomena as the diminutive her grandmother used for her youngest daughter.","zh":"她把成人语言学习形容为需要勇气、力量和自律的艰难情感劳动，尤其是在第一语言使用者不断流失、老人相继离世，而疫情又夺走了会话空间的时候。她说开口的恐惧与血脉记忆以及数代语言流失有关，这正是她必须克服的；祖母告诉她，犯错正是学习的方式。语言学帮助她解释了祖母曾用来称呼小女儿的指小形式这类现象。"},{"en":"Relations with the field of linguistics have not always been easy, and there is a lack of trust, partly because documentation was made when practising the language could be illegal. Claxton wants that documentation returned to communities in forms meaningful for revitalization. Her grandmother told her she lives in a different time, with access her ancestors were denied. She wants students to know that Indigenous peoples had a culture of understanding linguistics, that many spoke several languages without forcing others to conform, and that her language is alive.","zh":"她所在的社区与语言学领域的关系并不总是顺畅，彼此存在不信任，部分原因是当年的语言记录产生于使用这门语言甚至可能违法的年代。Claxton 希望这些记录回到社区，并以对复兴工作有意义的形式呈现。祖母曾告诉她，她生活在一个不同的时代，拥有祖先被剥夺的获取途径。她希望学生知道：原住民本来就有理解语言学的文化，许多人通晓多种语言却从不强迫他人顺从，而且她的语言是活的。"}]},"chapter-113-slug-9-9-growing-up-speaking-nishnaabemwin":{"title":{"en":"9.9 Growing up speaking Nishnaabemwin","zh":"9.9 在 Nishnaabemwin 的环境中长大"},"summary":{"en":"Mary Ann Corbiere grew up in Wiikwemkoong unceded territory, where Nishnaabemwin was the first language of every household in the 1960s. She learned English only in the classroom while speaking Nishnaabemwin with friends, which helped the language survive despite residential schooling. She later became a Nishnaabemwin teacher by chance, working out teaching patterns without formal linguistics training.","zh":"玛丽安·科尔比埃（Mary Ann Corbiere）在曼尼图林岛的 Wiikwemkoong 未割让领地长大，20 世纪 60 年代那里家家户户都以 Nishnaabemwin 为第一语言。她只在课堂上学英语，课间与放学后仍用本族语与伙伴交谈，这使该语言在寄宿学校冲击下得以延续。她后来偶然成为大学语言教师，在未受过语言学训练的情况下自行摸索教学规律。"},"points":[{"en":"Every household in Wiikwemkoong still spoke Nishnaabemwin as a first language when Corbiere began grade one in 1961.","zh":"1961 年科尔比埃上小学一年级时，Wiikwemkoong 的每家每户仍以 Nishnaabemwin 为第一语言。"},{"en":"Children used English only with teachers, and spoke Nishnaabemwin with friends at recess and after school.","zh":"孩子们在学校只对老师讲英语，课间和放学后与伙伴玩耍时则一律使用本族语。"},{"en":"She attributes the language's survival to critical mass: two months at home each year let residential school students regain it.","zh":"她把语言的延续归因于临界规模：寄宿学校的学生每年回家两个月便足以重新掌握语言。"},{"en":"She began teaching Nishnaabemwin in 1989 by happenstance, after majoring in Earth and Environmental Sciences and earning an MBA.","zh":"她 1989 年开始教 Nishnaabemwin，此前主修地球与环境科学，后来又念了 MBA。"},{"en":"With no linguistics training, she identified patterns herself, such as verb endings that vanish after short vowels but persist after long ones.","zh":"没有语言学训练，她自己看出规律，例如动词词尾在短元音后消失、在长元音后保留。"},{"en":"The university committed to distance delivery so First Nations students could train without relocating, and she wrote the correspondence course.","zh":"大学决定以远程方式开课，使第一民族学生不必搬家就能受训，函授课程由她编写。"}],"terms":[{"en":"first language","zh":"第一语言","defEn":"The language a child acquires first at home, before any later language learning.","defZh":"儿童在家庭环境中最早自然习得的语言，通常是日常交流中最常用的语言。"},{"en":"residential school","zh":"寄宿学校","defEn":"A government-run boarding school that separated Indigenous children from their families and languages.","defZh":"由政府开办、把原住民儿童与家庭和本族语隔离开来的寄宿学校。"},{"en":"critical mass","zh":"临界规模（critical mass）","defEn":"The idea that a sufficient number of speakers lets a language survive outside pressure.","defZh":"认为只要使用者达到一定数量，语言就能顶住外部压力而存续的观点。"},{"en":"mental grammar","zh":"心理语法","defEn":"A speaker's unconscious knowledge of the patterns of their language.","defZh":"说话人对自己语言规律的无意识知识，即心理语法。"},{"en":"distance course","zh":"远程课程","defEn":"A course delivered by mail or online rather than in a classroom.","defZh":"通过邮寄材料或网络而非课堂教学进行的课程，即远程课程。"}],"examples":[{"text":"wiisini becomes niwiisin","noteEn":"The verb for he is eating changes form to mean I am eating.","noteZh":"表示「他在吃」的动词改变形式后，表示「我在吃」。"},{"text":"I want to go skating tomorrow","noteEn":"One of the sentence patterns Corbiere uses to show what changes and what stays.","noteZh":"科尔比埃用来演示「什么变化、什么不变」的句型之一。"},{"text":"Wiki is what we call Wiikwemkoong for short","noteEn":"Corbiere's own gloss on the short name for her home community.","noteZh":"科尔比埃对家乡社区简称的说明。"}],"body":[{"en":"Corbiere comes from Wiikwemkoong unceded territory at the east end of Manitoulin Island, one of the largest Nishnaabe communities in Ontario. When she entered grade one in 1961, every household still spoke Nishnaabemwin as its first language, and she considers herself fortunate to have grown up then. English was used only with teachers in the classroom; at recess in the school yard and after school with friends, everyone spoke the language.","zh":"科尔比埃来自曼尼图林岛东端的 Wiikwemkoong 未割让领地，这是安大略省最大的尼什纳贝（Nishnaabe）社区之一。1961 年她上小学一年级时，社区里家家户户都还说 Nishnaabemwin，那是他们的第一语言，她觉得自己赶上了好时候。在学校里，他们只用英语与老师交流；一到课间在操场、放学后和伙伴玩耍，大家说的都是本族语。"},{"en":"Although her parents' and grandparents' generation had attended the residential school at Spanish, the language survived in homes. She suggests the explanation may lie in critical mass: children were away roughly September to June, yet two months back home were apparently enough to restore the language and keep it up. Wiikwemkoong and the somewhat smaller community of M'Chigeeng both kept it in daily household use.","zh":"尽管她父母和祖父母那一代曾在 Spanish 的寄宿学校就读，这门语言仍然在家庭中存活下来。她猜测原因在于临界规模：孩子们大约九月到次年六月在外读书，但回家两个月似乎就足以把语言捡回来并保持下去。Wiikwemkoong 和岛上稍小的 M'Chigeeng 两个社区都是如此。"},{"en":"After majoring in Earth and Environmental Sciences and then completing an MBA, she returned home and was drawn into teaching through a chance interview for a Native Social Work program, where she was asked whether she would also teach the language. She had no linguistics background, but Richard Rhodes's 1985 dictionary and Piggott's work on sound patterns helped her. She worked out for herself why verb endings change: endings with short vowels disappear in certain forms, while long vowels remain.","zh":"她上大学主修地球与环境科学，之后又读了 MBA，回到家乡后因一次偶然的面试进入原住民社会工作项目，被问到能否顺便教语言，就这样答应了，尽管她从未想过自己会教大学。她没有语言学背景，理查德·罗兹 1985 年的词典和皮戈特的研究帮了她。她还自己琢磨出动词词尾变化的道理：以短元音结尾的词尾在某些形式中消失，长元音则保留。"},{"en":"Instead of having students memorize whole dialogues, she built her course around patterns, asking them to find what stays the same and what must be replaced. When the Native social work program committed to distance delivery, she wrote the Ojibwe correspondence course with an instructional designer, and later created an intermediate course and expanded the distance offerings, since learners cannot jump straight from introductory to advanced.","zh":"她不再让学生背整段对话，而是围绕句型教学，让学生找出什么保持不变、什么需要替换。原住民社会工作项目决定以远程方式开课后，她与教学设计者合作编写了奥吉布瓦语函授课程，后来又增设中级课程并扩大远程课程，因为学习者无法从入门直接跳到高级。"}]},"chapter-114-slug-9-10-learning-nishnaabemwin-at-university":{"title":{"en":"9.10 Learning Nishnaabemwin at University","zh":"9.10 在大学学习 Nishnaabemwin"},"summary":{"en":"Corbiere describes how her university classes were roughly half Native and half non-Native students, and how new degree requirements pushed enrollment above forty, forcing a second section. Bigger, more mixed classes brought tensions: some students assumed an easy course, while some Native students expected the language to come easily and struggled. Written assessment exposed painful questions of identity, and many learners were reconnecting with a language their families had lost.","zh":"科尔比埃描述她的大学课堂大致一半是原住民学生、一半非原住民学生；学位要求修改后选课人数超过四十人，学校不得不增开一个班。班级更大、成分更杂也带来张力：有的学生以为课程轻松，有的原住民学生以为凭血统语言自然不难，结果学得吃力。笔试还把身份认同的难题暴露出来，许多学习者其实是在重新连接家人失去的语言。"},"points":[{"en":"Students came for a Native Studies concentration, the Native Social Work program, or general degree requirements.","zh":"学生的选课动机包括原住民研究方向、原住民社会工作项目，或只是满足学位要求。"},{"en":"Enrollment was typically half Native and half non-Native students, a pattern that held from the start.","zh":"选课学生通常一半是原住民、一半非原住民，这一格局从她开始教书起就未变。"},{"en":"New requirements in Indigenous content and linguistic awareness let one course serve both, and over forty students registered.","zh":"新增的原住民内容与语言意识要求使一门课可同时满足两项，注册人数超过四十人。"},{"en":"Language learning involves pronunciation, comprehension, reading and writing, and few learners are strong in all four.","zh":"语言学习包含发音、理解、阅读和写作，很少有人四项都强。"},{"en":"Written tests hurt, because some non-Native students with a knack for languages outscored struggling Native classmates.","zh":"笔试令人难受：一些有语言天赋的非原住民学生分数高于苦苦挣扎的原住民同学。"},{"en":"Some communities distrust linguists because of a history of knowledge extraction that credited scholars rather than contributors.","zh":"一些社区因知识榨取的历史而不信任语言学家：署名的是学者，提供者只被称作报道人。"}],"terms":[{"en":"second language","zh":"第二语言","defEn":"A language learned after the first language, often in school or later life.","defZh":"在习得第一语言之后学习的语言，多通过学校教育或后天接触掌握。"},{"en":"aptitude","zh":"语言学能（aptitude）","defEn":"An individual's natural ease or difficulty in learning a particular skill such as a language.","defZh":"个人学习语言等技能时表现出的难易倾向，即语言学能。"},{"en":"four skills","zh":"四项技能","defEn":"Pronunciation, listening comprehension, reading comprehension and writing, which learners develop unevenly.","defZh":"发音、听力理解、阅读理解与写作四项能力，学习者的水平往往并不均衡。"},{"en":"knowledge extraction","zh":"知识榨取","defEn":"Research in which outside scholars take Indigenous knowledge and receive the credit.","defZh":"外部学者获取原住民知识并独占学术声誉的做法，即知识榨取。"},{"en":"language revitalization","zh":"语言复兴","defEn":"Efforts to restore a language's use after it has declined or stopped being passed on.","defZh":"语言衰落或不再代际传承之后，为恢复其使用而作的各种努力。"}],"examples":[{"text":"This is the first time I’ve seen the language, explained, shown in that way.","noteEn":"A student's comment on the patterned presentation of the language in Corbiere's course.","noteZh":"一名学生对科尔比埃课程以句型方式呈现语言的评论。"},{"text":"I have to take this thing and this is the only thing I can fit in my timetable","noteEn":"The attitude of students taking the course only because it fits their schedule.","noteZh":"只因为时间表排得下才选这门课的学生心态。"},{"text":"I won’t need to have to work as hard on this one because I’m Native, I’m Ojibwe, I’m Nishnaabe.","noteEn":"The expectation Corbiere sometimes detected among Native students about the language coming easily.","noteZh":"科尔比埃有时在原住民学生身上察觉到的「凭血统自然学得轻松」的期待。"}],"body":[{"en":"Students took Corbiere's courses for several reasons: a concentration in Native Studies, the Native Social Work program, or general degree requirements. Roughly half were Native and half non-Native, a pattern that held from the beginning. Enrollment rose sharply when Laurentian modified its BA requirements, so that all students needed a course designated as Indigenous content and most also needed one in linguistic awareness; they realized a single Nishnaabemwin course could satisfy both. Over forty students registered, and the university created a second section at short notice.","zh":"选修科尔比埃课程的学生来源多样：有人为原住民研究专业的方向课而来，有人来自原住民社会工作项目，也有人只是为了满足学位要求。学生背景大致是一半原住民、一半非原住民，这一格局从她开始教书起就没有变过。劳伦森大学修改学士学位要求、规定所有学生都要修一门原住民内容课程以及一门语言意识课程后，选课人数激增，学生们发现一门 Nishnaabemwin 课就能同时满足两项要求，注册人数超过四十人，学校只好临时增开一个班。"},{"en":"Bigger classes changed the dynamics. Some students arrived with negative preconceptions, taking the course only because it fit their timetable, or assuming it would be easy. At the same time, some Native students expected the language to come readily because of their heritage, and struggled when it did not. Corbiere also notes that language learning involves four skills, pronunciation, comprehension, reading and writing, and that few learners are equally strong in all of them.","zh":"班级变大后，课堂气氛也变了。有些学生带着负面成见而来，只是因为时间表排得下才选这门课，或者以为这是一门轻松的课。与此同时，一些原住民学生以为凭自己的血统语言自然不在话下，结果学得很吃力。科尔比埃还指出，语言学习包含发音、理解、阅读和写作四项技能，很少有人四方面同样出色。"},{"en":"Written tests exposed painful tensions, since some non-Native students with a knack for languages earned high marks while Native classmates struggled. For many Native students the course was not a linguistic curiosity but a way to reconnect with an identity that had been cut off, because their parents had not learned the language, or because small communities bused children to non-Native schools from the start. Corbiere says this is the hardest part of teaching.","zh":"笔试成绩暴露出令人难受的矛盾：一些有语言天赋的非原住民学生考得很好，而部分原住民同学却成绩不佳。对许多原住民学生来说，这门课不是语言学上的好奇，而是重新连接被割断的身份——他们的父母没有学会这门语言，或者所在的小社区很早就把孩子送到非原住民的学校就读。科尔比埃说这是她教学中最难面对的部分。"},{"en":"She describes how an instructional designer, Cheryl Cranley, coached her into a repeated template: examples, explanation, a short practice activity, then a self test. K to 12 teachers, by contrast, are overloaded, lack resources and often have little linguistics background. She also notes that some communities distrust linguists because of the history of knowledge extraction, in which scholars put their names on books while Native contributors were acknowledged as informants; others assume Elders are the best teachers, though fluency alone does not supply teaching skill.","zh":"她讲到教学设计者谢丽尔·克兰利如何带她形成固定的课程模板：先给例子，再解释，然后安排一个小练习，最后附上自测。相比之下，K 到 12 的教师负担过重、缺乏资源，往往也没有语言学背景。她还提到，一些社区对语言学家心存戒备，因为历史上学者把原住民知识写进书里，署名的是学者，提供者只被称作「报道人」；另一些人则认为长者才是最好的老师，但能流利说话并不等于会教。"}]},"chapter-115-slug-9-11-resources-for-teaching-and-learning-nishnaabemwin":{"title":{"en":"9.11 Resources for teaching and learning Nishnaabemwin","zh":"9.11 Nishnaabemwin 的教学与学习资源"},"summary":{"en":"Corbiere argues that curricula often reduce Indigenous culture to a static, pre-contact authenticity, focusing on beadwork and moccasins while ignoring farming, mining, hockey and quilting, for which the language has vocabulary. She teaches patterns rather than fixed word lists so learners can communicate about their own lives. Since speakers are few, she expects the language to survive largely in writing, and she discusses the writing system, inconsistent spelling, and the difficulty of coordinating teachers across Ontario.","zh":"科尔比埃认为，课程常常把原住民文化压缩成静止的、接触前的「本真」形象，只讲珠饰和莫卡辛鞋，却忽略农耕、采矿、冰球和绗被——这些在语言中都有词。她教句型而非固定词表，让学习者能谈论自己的生活。由于使用者稀少，她预计这门语言将主要靠书面形式存续，并讨论了书写系统、拼写不统一以及安大略各地教师难以协调的问题。"},"points":[{"en":"Culture in Indigenous curricula is often treated as static pre-contact authenticity, reduced to beadwork and moccasin-making.","zh":"原住民课程中的「文化」常被当成静止的接触前本真性，简化为珠饰与做莫卡辛鞋。"},{"en":"Community life today includes hockey, baseball, farming and mining, and the lexicon has terms for all of them.","zh":"今天的社区生活包括冰球、棒球、农耕和采矿，词库里对这些都是有词的。"},{"en":"Corbiere teaches patterns instead of a fixed verb list, so learners can talk about what interests them, such as quilting.","zh":"科尔比埃教句型而非固定动词表，让学习者能谈论自己感兴趣的事，比如绗被。"},{"en":"She criticizes K to 12 word-list materials, since knowing terms like bear or birch bark is not communicating.","zh":"她批评 K 到 12 的词表式教材：认识「熊」「桦树皮」不等于能交流。"},{"en":"Because speakers are few, she thinks Nishnaabemwin may survive largely as a written language.","zh":"由于使用者稀少，她认为 Nishnaabemwin 可能主要作为书面语言存续。"},{"en":"Spelling varies across communities, and teachers seldom have time to agree on applying one system to longer discourse.","zh":"各地拼写不一致，教师也很少有时间商定如何把同一套系统用于更长语篇。"}],"terms":[{"en":"material culture","zh":"物质文化","defEn":"The physical objects a community makes and uses, such as moccasins or beadwork.","defZh":"一个社群制作和使用的实物，如莫卡辛鞋、珠饰等，即物质文化。"},{"en":"polysynthetic","zh":"多式综合（polysynthetic）","defEn":"A language type in which many morphemes combine into a single long word.","defZh":"一种语言类型，其特点是把许多语素组合进一个很长的词里。"},{"en":"double vowel system","zh":"双元音书写系统","defEn":"A practical orthography that writes long vowels with two letters, used for Ojibwe.","defZh":"用两个字母表示长元音的一套实用正写法，广泛用于奥吉布瓦语。"},{"en":"lexicon","zh":"词库","defEn":"The set of words and word-forming elements available in a language.","defZh":"一种语言中全部的词及构词成分的总和，即词库。"},{"en":"grammar-translation method","zh":"语法翻译法","defEn":"A teaching method emphasizing rules and translation rather than spoken communication.","defZh":"强调语法规则和翻译练习、忽视口语交际的教学法，即语法翻译法。"}],"examples":[{"text":"badakiiga`ige","noteEn":"The verb Corbiere cites for quilting, showing the language covers modern activities.","noteZh":"科尔比埃举出的表示绗被的动词，说明该语言能谈论现代活动。"},{"text":"mko","noteEn":"The word for bear, which she has also seen written as makwa and mako.","noteZh":"表示「熊」的词，她还见过 makwa 和 mako 两种写法。"},{"text":"They were skunked.","noteEn":"One of the hockey verbs Corbiere says exist in the Nishnaabemwin lexicon.","noteZh":"科尔比埃说 Nishnaabemwin 词库里存在的冰球说法之一。"}],"body":[{"en":"Corbiere questions how culture is defined for Indigenous language curricula. European-based culture suggests the arts, film, theatre and opera, but culture applied to Indigenous peoples becomes a static measure of authenticity tied to a pre-contact traditional life. That framing pushes classrooms toward moccasin-making and beadwork, though not everyone does those things. Her own vocabulary came from farming; others hunted and fished, and still others worked in mines, for which Nishnaabemwin has terms, including one for going on strike.","zh":"科尔比埃质疑原住民语言课程对「文化」的定义。欧洲文化让人想到艺术、电影、戏剧和歌剧，而「文化」一旦用在原住民身上，就变成一种静态的「本真性」标准，指向接触前的「传统」生活方式。这种框架把课堂引向做莫卡辛鞋和珠饰，可这些并不是人人都会做。她自己的词汇储备来自农耕，因为父亲是农夫；别人家打猎、捕鱼，还有人去矿上工作，而 Nishnaabemwin 里有采矿的词，也有罢工的词。"},{"en":"Community life also includes hockey and baseball, and the lexicon has verbs for scoring a goal or being beaten and skunked. Corbiere therefore refuses to dictate a fixed verb list: she teaches patterns so learners can discuss what matters to them, whether figure skating or quilting, for which the verb badakiiga`ige exists. She criticizes K to 12 materials built on word lists such as bear and birch bark, since knowing terms is not the same as communicating. Revitalization, she argues, requires using the language and adapting it to new needs, such as asking for a ream of printer paper.","zh":"今天的社区生活还包括冰球和棒球，词库里就有表示进球、被打败、被剃光头的动词。因此科尔比埃拒绝规定一份固定的动词表，而是教句型，让学习者能谈论自己关心的事，无论是花样滑冰还是绗被，后者有动词 badakiiga`ige。她批评 K 到 12 教材以词表为主，例如「熊」「桦树皮」，因为认识词不等于能交流。她认为语言复兴必须真正使用它，并让它适应新事物，比如要一叠打印纸。"},{"en":"Online tools such as email and Zoom let scattered learners form a new kind of community not rooted in blood kinship, raising the question of what the Nishnaabemwin speech community will look like in fifteen years. Because many communities now have only a handful of speakers, Corbiere expects the language may survive largely in written form. She uses the writing system devised by Fiero, which marks long vowels, following Piggott's finding that long vowels take primary accent while paired short vowels may be destressed. Spelling still varies: the word for bear appears as mko, makwa or mako.","zh":"电子邮件和 Zoom 让分散各地的学习者形成一种不以血缘为基础的新社群，这也引出十五年后 Nishnaabemwin 言语社群会是什么样子的问题。由于许多社区只剩四五位使用者，她认为这门语言很可能主要以书面形式存续。她使用菲耶罗设计的书写系统，标出长元音；皮戈特的分析表明长元音承担主重音，成对的短元音则可能失去重音。拼写仍不统一，「熊」就有 mko、makwa、mako 等写法。"},{"en":"Because teachers are scattered across Ontario and seldom have time to confer, applying the system consistently to longer discourse remains difficult, and no one wants to declare themselves the authority on correctness. Resistance to writing, limited time, and an outdated grammar-translation curriculum add to the problem.","zh":"由于教师分散在安大略各地，难得聚在一起长时间商议，把书写系统一致地用于更长语篇仍然困难，也没有人愿意自封为正确性的权威。对书写的抵触、时间的限制，以及过时的语法翻译法课程，更让问题雪上加霜。"}]},"part-010-chapter-10-language-variation-and-change":{"title":{"en":"Chapter 10: Language Variation and Change","zh":"第 10 章：语言变异与变化"},"summary":{"en":"Chapter 10 introduces sociolinguistic variation and the ways linguists have analyzed it. Readers meet cross-linguistic and sociolinguistic variation, learn to recognize linguistic variables in data, survey major social factors that correlate with variation, and practice interpreting quantitative sociolinguistic graphs.","zh":"第 10 章介绍社会语言学变异及学界对其的分析方法。读者将认识跨语言变异与社会语言学变异的区别，学习在语料中识别语言变项，了解与变异相关的主要社会因素，并练习解读定量社会语言学图表。"},"points":[{"en":"Explain how cross-linguistic variation differs from sociolinguistic variation.","zh":"说明跨语言变异与社会语言学变异之间的差别。"},{"en":"Define the important concepts that sociolinguists rely on when studying variation.","zh":"界定社会语言学家研究变异时所依赖的重要概念。"},{"en":"Recognize linguistic variables as they appear in real language data.","zh":"在真实语言语料中识别出语言变项。"},{"en":"Discuss some of the major findings that variationist sociolinguistics has produced.","zh":"讨论变异社会语言学取得的一些主要发现。"},{"en":"Interpret basic patterns in quantitative sociolinguistic data presented in graphs.","zh":"解读以图表呈现的定量社会语言学数据中的基本模式。"}],"terms":[{"en":"linguistic variation","zh":"语言变异","defEn":"Different ways of saying the same thing within or across languages.","defZh":"在同一语言内部或不同语言之间，表达同一意思的不同方式。"},{"en":"variationist sociolinguistics","zh":"变异社会语言学","defEn":"The approach that analyzes how language varies with its context of use.","defZh":"分析语言如何随使用语境而变化的语言学取向。"},{"en":"social factor","zh":"社会因素","defEn":"A speaker or situation property that correlates with linguistic variation.","defZh":"与语言变异相关的说话人或情境属性。"},{"en":"quantitative sociolinguistic data","zh":"定量社会语言学数据","defEn":"Counted variant frequencies that are summarized and displayed in graphs.","defZh":"经过计数的变式频率，通常以图表汇总和呈现。"}],"examples":[],"body":[{"en":"This chapter turns to sociolinguistic variation and the ways linguists have gone about analyzing it. It opens with the notion of linguistic variation, then shows how data are handled from a variationist sociolinguistic perspective. Along the way it surveys several of the social factors that are known to correlate with the way people vary their language.","zh":"本章转向社会语言学变异，以及语言学家分析这种变异的方式。开头先引出语言变异这一概念，接着展示如何从变异社会语言学的视角处理数据。行文中还考察了若干已知与人们语言变异方式相关的社会因素。"},{"en":"By the end of the chapter, readers should be able to separate cross-linguistic variation from sociolinguistic variation, define the key concepts of sociolinguistics, spot linguistic variables in language data, summarize major findings of variationist research, and read basic patterns out of graphs of quantitative sociolinguistic data.","zh":"读完本章，读者应能区分跨语言变异与社会语言学变异，界定社会语言学的核心概念，在语言语料中发现语言变项，概括变异研究的主要发现，并从定量社会语言学数据的图表中读出基本模式。"}]},"chapter-116-slug-10-1-what-is-variationist-sociolinguistics":{"title":{"en":"10.1 What is variationist sociolinguistics?","zh":"10.1 什么是变异社会语言学？"},"summary":{"en":"Variationist sociolinguistics is a methodological and analytical approach to the relationship between language and its context of use. It asks questions such as why some Canadian English speakers say eh while others say right, and treats social and linguistic factors as equally important. It contrasts with formal subfields that study abstract competence rather than actual performance.","zh":"变异社会语言学是一种研究语言与其使用语境之间关系的方法论与分析路径。它追问诸如为何有些加拿大英语使用者说 eh、另一些说 right 之类的问题，并认为社会因素与语言因素同等重要。它与研究抽象语言能力的各种形式子学科形成对照，关注的是实际语言运用而非抽象知识。"},"points":[{"en":"Variationist sociolinguistics relates language to its context of use.","zh":"变异社会语言学把语言与其使用语境联系起来考察。"},{"en":"Social and linguistic factors matter equally in this approach.","zh":"在这一研究路径中，社会因素与语言因素同等重要。"},{"en":"The field is defined by its concern with the variable nature of language in use.","zh":"该领域的界定特征在于关注使用中语言的变异性本质。"},{"en":"Formal subfields like phonology and syntax focus on abstract, impersonal grammar.","zh":"音系学、句法学等形式子学科关注抽象的、非个人的语法。"},{"en":"Competence is abstract language knowledge; performance is practical language use.","zh":"语言能力是抽象的语言知识；语言运用是实际的语言使用。"}],"terms":[{"en":"variationist sociolinguistics","zh":"变异社会语言学","defEn":"A methodological and analytical approach linking language to its context of use.","defZh":"把语言与其使用语境联系起来的方法论与分析路径。"},{"en":"context of use","zh":"使用语境","defEn":"The social and situational circumstances in which language is actually used.","defZh":"语言实际被使用时的社会与情境环境。"},{"en":"competence","zh":"语言能力","defEn":"The abstract language knowledge that our mind possesses.","defZh":"我们心智所拥有的抽象语言知识。"},{"en":"performance","zh":"语言运用","defEn":"The practical, actual use of language by speakers.","defZh":"说话人对语言实际而具体的运用。"},{"en":"competence/performance distinction","zh":"语言能力／语言运用之分","defEn":"The contrast between abstract language knowledge and practical language use.","defZh":"抽象语言知识与实际语言运用之间的区分。"}],"examples":[{"text":"“It’s cold out, eh?” “It’s cold out, right?”","noteEn":"Canadian English speakers vary between eh and right in the same slot.","noteZh":"加拿大英语使用者在同一位置上会在 eh 与 right 之间变换。"},{"text":"What kinds of information do we assume about them?","noteEn":"A variationist question about what a variant reveals about a speaker.","noteZh":"这是一个变异社会语言学问题：某个变式透露出说话人的什么信息。"}],"body":[{"en":"Why is it that some speakers of Canadian English close their sentences with eh, while others choose right? In which situations is a given person more likely to use one rather than the other? What do listeners assume about someone on the basis of hearing eh, right, or innit? And have such patterns shifted over time? These are the sorts of questions variationist sociolinguistics sets out to answer.","zh":"为什么有些加拿大英语使用者用 eh 收尾，另一些人却选择 right？在什么情境下，同一个人更可能用其中一个而非另一个？听到 eh、right 或 innit 时，听话人会据此对说话人作出怎样的推断？这些模式是否随时间发生了改变？这些正是变异社会语言学试图回答的问题。"},{"en":"Variationist sociolinguistics is a methodological and analytical approach to understanding the relationship between language and its context of use. The socio- part of the name signals that social and linguistic factors carry equal weight in this field. The variationist part signals that the object of study is the variable nature of language as it is actually used.","zh":"变异社会语言学是一种理解语言与其使用语境之间关系的方法论与分析路径。名称中的 socio- 表明，社会因素与语言因素在这一领域中分量相当；variationist 则表明，研究对象是语言在实际使用中呈现的变异性质。"},{"en":"The formal subfields of linguistics, such as phonology and syntax, tend to concentrate on an abstract, impersonal grammar: everything our mind knows about our language. Sociolinguistics, by contrast, analyzes language as people really use it in practice. Some linguists label these two ways of studying language the competence/performance distinction, where competence is the abstract knowledge and performance is the practical use.","zh":"语言学中的形式子学科，如音系学和句法学，倾向于聚焦一种抽象的、非个人的语法，即我们心智关于自身语言所知道的一切。与此相对，社会语言学分析的是人们在实践中真实使用的语言。有些语言学家把这两种研究语言的方式称为语言能力／语言运用之分，其中语言能力指抽象知识，语言运用指实际使用。"}]},"chapter-117-slug-10-2-language-varies":{"title":{"en":"10.2 Language varies","zh":"10.2 语言存在变异"},"summary":{"en":"Every language and variety shows variation, in all modalities and at every level from phonetics to pragmatics. A linguistic variable is an abstract set of options, and the options speakers choose are variants. Variation must be distinguished from synonyms with different connotations and from deterministic categorical alternations; linguistic variables are probabilistic rather than random.","zh":"每一种语言和变体都存在变异，涉及所有模态以及从语音到语用的各个层面。语言变项是一组抽象的选项，说话人实际选用的选项就是变式。变异须与涵义不同的同义词、以及与语言环境严格对应的范畴性交替区分开来；语言变项具有概率性，而并非随机。"},"points":[{"en":"Everyone has a dialect, and a standard language is merely the prestigious one.","zh":"人人都有自己的方言，所谓标准语不过是社会声望更高的那一种。"},{"en":"Cross-linguistic variation appears between languages, as with Icelandic and Danish negation order.","zh":"跨语言变异出现在语言之间，如冰岛语与丹麦语否定词序的差异。"},{"en":"Linguistic variables occur in all modalities and at all domains, from phonetic to pragmatic.","zh":"语言变项存在于所有模态和所有层面，从语音层面到语用层面。"},{"en":"Variants of a variable all do the same thing, unlike synonyms with differing connotations.","zh":"同一变项的各个变式功能相同，这与涵义各异的同义词不同。"},{"en":"Categorical alternations are deterministic, while linguistic variables are probabilistic.","zh":"范畴性交替是确定性的，而语言变项则是概率性的。"}],"terms":[{"en":"variety","zh":"语言变体","defEn":"A subdivision of a language, used without the negative connotations of dialect.","defZh":"语言的次类划分，使用时不像“方言”一词带有贬义色彩。"},{"en":"cross-linguistic variation","zh":"跨语言变异","defEn":"Two languages having different ways of doing the same thing.","defZh":"两种语言对同一件事采用不同处理方式的现象。"},{"en":"sociolinguistic variation","zh":"社会语言学变异","defEn":"Two or more ways of doing the same thing within one language.","defZh":"在同一语言内部，对同一件事存在两种或更多处理方式。"},{"en":"linguistic variable","zh":"语言变项","defEn":"The abstract set of options available when more than one way exists.","defZh":"当同一功能存在多种表达方式时，可供选择的抽象选项集合。"},{"en":"variant","zh":"变式","defEn":"One individual option that a language user actually chooses.","defZh":"语言使用者实际选用的某一个具体选项。"},{"en":"categorical alternation","zh":"范畴性交替","defEn":"A deterministic choice that depends strictly on the linguistic context.","defZh":"严格取决于语言环境、具有确定性的选择。"}],"examples":[{"text":"Saints do not move, though grant for prayers sake. Then move not while my prayers effect I take.","noteEn":"Shakespeare's couplet shows both negation orders within one writer.","noteZh":"莎士比亚的这一对偶句显示同一位作者并用两种否定词序。"},{"text":"包 bao [paw] 包儿 baor [pawr]","noteEn":"A syllable without a coda may be variably rhoticized in Beijing Mandarin.","noteZh":"北京话中无韵尾的音节可以可变地发生儿化。"},{"text":"an apple [ənæpl] and a apple [əʔæpl]","noteEn":"London English varies between an and a before a vowel.","noteZh":"伦敦英语在元音前会在 an 与 a 之间发生变异。"},{"text":"I’m fishing this morning.","noteEn":"The ending may surface as [ɪŋ] or as [ɪn], conditioned by category.","noteZh":"词尾可以读作 [ɪŋ] 或 [ɪn]，并受词类因素制约。"}],"body":[{"en":"In everyday usage the word dialect often carries the suggestion that a way of speaking is substandard, low status, geographically isolated, or a deviation from some standard. But every speaker has a dialect, and a so-called standard language is not linguistically superior; it simply carries more social prestige. Linguistics does not rank dialects hierarchically, and because variety lacks those negative connotations, subdivisions of a language are called varieties here.","zh":"日常使用中，“方言”一词常暗示某种说话方式低劣、地位不高、地域封闭，或偏离了某种“标准”。但每位说话人都拥有方言，所谓标准语在语言上并不更优越，只是附带更多社会声望而已。语言学并不把方言排成等级，而 variety 一词不带这些负面含义，因此本章把语言的次类划分称为语言变体。"},{"en":"Comparing forms across languages reveals cross-linguistic variation. Icelandic and Danish both express the meaning of I didn't ask why Peter hadn't read it, yet they order the negative marker differently in the embedded clause: Icelandic has hafði ekki whereas Danish has ikke havde. Strikingly, the same two options coexisted inside one variety of Early Modern English, as Shakespeare's rhyming couplet about moving and praying shows, with one character using VERB-NEGATIVE and the other NEGATIVE-VERB. That is sociolinguistic variation.","zh":"把不同语言的形式拿来比较，就能看到跨语言变异。冰岛语和丹麦语都能表达“我没问彼得为什么没读它”这一意思，但在嵌入小句“彼得没读它”中，否定标记与动词的先后顺序不同：冰岛语说成 hafði ekki，即“有—不”；丹麦语说成 ikke havde，即“不—有”。也就是说，两种语言用两种不同的方式做同一件事。值得注意的是，同样的两种选项也曾共存于早期现代英语的同一变体之中，莎士比亚那句关于移动与祈祷的对偶句即为明证：同一段押韵对句中，两个人物分别使用动词—否定与否定—动词两种语序。这种在同一语言、同一变体乃至同一位作者内部并存的两种做法，就是社会语言学变异。"},{"en":"When more than one way exists to do the same thing, that is a linguistic variable, and the individual options users select are its variants. The variable itself is an abstract set that we never observe directly; we only see it when someone produces a variant. Variables appear in every language and variety, in every modality, and at every level of grammar. Auslan, New Zealand Sign Language and American Sign Language vary the location of signs such as KNOW, THINK and NAME; Beijing Mandarin variably rhoticizes codaless syllables; London English alternates between an and a before vowels; and North Baffin Inuktitut transitives vary between ergative and antipassive alignment.","zh":"当同一件事存在不止一种做法时，便构成语言变项，使用者所选的具体选项就是变式。变项本身是一个抽象的集合，我们从不直接观察它，只有某人说出某个变式时才见到它的踪影。变项出现在所有语言和变体、所有模态以及语法的各个层面。澳大利亚手语、新西兰手语和美国手语中，KNOW、THINK、NAME 等手语词的位置可变；北京话无韵尾音节可以发生儿化；伦敦英语在元音前交替使用 an 与 a；北巴芬因纽特语的及物结构则在作格与反被动两种配列间变异。"},{"en":"Not every alternation counts as a linguistic variable. Synonyms such as car, automobile, ride, jalopy, hooptie and paddock basher denote the same kind of vehicle, yet they differ in connotation and in regional or social use, so they are not interchangeable. Categorical alternations, such as the [z], [s] and extra-vowel forms of the English plural, depend strictly on the linguistic environment and are deterministic. Linguistic variables instead vary within the same environment, and their variants are chosen probabilistically. As one foundational study put it, there is order amid the heterogeneity.","zh":"并非所有交替都算语言变项。car、automobile、ride、jalopy、hooptie、paddock basher 等词指称同一种车辆，但涵义及地域或社会用法各异，因而不能随意互换。范畴性交替，如英语复数后缀的 [z]、[s] 与加元音三种形式，严格取决于语言环境，具有确定性。语言变项则能在同一环境内部发生变异，其变式的选择具有概率性。正如一项奠基性研究所言，异质之中自有秩序。"}]},"chapter-118-slug-10-3-language-changes":{"title":{"en":"10.3 Language changes","zh":"10.3 语言变化"},"summary":{"en":"All languages change over time, and change always brings a period of sociolinguistic variation, since old and new variants coexist while one gradually displaces the other. Sociolinguists analyze change through real time and apparent time comparisons and distinguish changes from above, driven by social awareness, from changes from below, driven by internal pressures.","zh":"所有语言都在不断变化，而变化过程中必然伴随社会语言学的变异，因为新旧变式会在一段时期内并存，随后一方逐步取代另一方。社会语言学家用实时分析与显时分析来考察变化，并把发生于社会意识层面的来自上方的变化与源于系统内部压力的来自下方的变化区分开来。"},"points":[{"en":"English, Japanese and all languages keep changing, so today's speakers differ from those of earlier centuries.","zh":"英语、日语以及所有语言都在持续变化，今人的语言不同于数百年前作者的用法。"},{"en":"Every change in progress passes through a period of variation, though stable variation need not lead to change.","zh":"进行中的变化都要经历变异阶段，但稳定变异并不必然导致语言变化。"},{"en":"Real time analysis compares the same community at two periods; apparent time analysis compares older and younger speakers.","zh":"实时分析比较同一社区在两个时期的数据；显时分析则比较年长者与年轻者的语言。"},{"en":"Apparent time rests on the claim that individual grammars stabilize in late adolescence, around age 18.","zh":"显时分析依据的是个人语法在青春期后期（约十八岁）趋于稳定的论断。"},{"en":"Changes from above occur above social awareness and usually import a prestigious or standardized variant.","zh":"来自上方的变化发生在社会意识层面之上，通常引入有威望或标准化的变式。"},{"en":"Changes from below arise from articulatory or grammatical pressures that speakers are generally unaware of.","zh":"来自下方的变化源于发音或语法系统的内部压力，说话人通常意识不到。"}],"terms":[{"en":"linguistic variable","zh":"语言变项","defEn":"A linguistic item with variants whose choice is subject to social and linguistic conditioning.","defZh":"具有多个变式、其选择受社会与语言因素制约的语言项目。"},{"en":"real time analysis","zh":"实时分析","defEn":"Comparing data representing the same community at two different periods of time.","defZh":"比较代表同一言语社区在两个不同时期的数据，以判断变化是否发生。"},{"en":"apparent time analysis","zh":"显时分析","defEn":"Inferring change by comparing the language of older and younger speakers in one period.","defZh":"在同一时期比较年长与年轻说话人的语言，从而推断语言变化。"},{"en":"change from above","zh":"来自上方的变化","defEn":"A change taking place above the level of social awareness, often adopting a prestigious variant.","defZh":"发生在社会意识层面之上的变化，常表现为采用社区外有威望的变式。"},{"en":"change from below","zh":"来自下方的变化","defEn":"A change driven by articulatory or grammatical pressures speakers are generally unaware of.","defZh":"由发音或语法系统内部压力驱动的变化，说话人一般并未察觉。"},{"en":"stable variation","zh":"稳定变异","defEn":"A situation where two or more variants coexist without one replacing the other.","defZh":"两个或多个变式并存、彼此并未相互取代的变异状态。"}],"examples":[{"text":"[kʰɒət] and [stɒə]","noteEn":"The older r-less pronunciations of cart and star in New York City English.","noteZh":"纽约市英语中 cart 与 star 较早的无 r 发音。"},{"text":"[kʰɒɹt] and [stɒɹ]","noteEn":"The newer, prestigious r-full variants that spread into New York City English.","noteZh":"后来进入纽约市英语、带威望的 r 音变式。"},{"text":"[u] ... [ʉ] or even [y]","noteEn":"The Canadian English goose vowel moving frontward in the vowel space.","noteZh":"加拿大英语 goose 元音在元音空间中不断前移。"},{"text":"move not","noteEn":"The older English negative pattern gradually replaced by do not move.","noteZh":"英语中较旧的否定形式，逐渐被 do not move 取代。"}],"body":[{"en":"All languages change over time. English speakers today do not talk like the authors of Beowulf, The Canterbury Tales or Hamlet, and Japanese speakers likewise differ from the authors of the Kojiki and the Genji Monogatari. Every language has changed and continues to evolve. Change matters to variationist sociolinguistics because variation always accompanies change in progress: the shift from move not to do not move was gradual, and during the transition both options were variants of one linguistic variable. Stable variation allows variants to coexist without replacement, but every change in progress passes through a period of sociolinguistic variation.","zh":"所有语言都在随时间变化。今天的英语使用者不会像《贝奥武夫》《坎特伯雷故事集》或《哈姆雷特》的作者那样说话，今天的日语使用者也不同于《古事记》和《源氏物语》的作者。每种语言都已改变，并且仍在演变。变化对变异社会语言学很重要，因为进行中的变化总是伴随着变异：从 move not 到 do not move 的转变是渐进的，过渡期内两种形式是同一个语言变项的变式。稳定变异允许若干变式并存而不相互取代，但每一种进行中的变化都必然经历一个社会语言学变异期。"},{"en":"To analyze change, researchers may use real time analysis, comparing data from the same community at two different periods; a difference in variant frequency indicates that a change has taken or is taking place. When older data are unavailable, apparent time analysis compares older and younger people instead, relying on the observation that grammars stabilize in late adolescence. The variable patterning we have at about 18 generally stays with us, even though we keep learning new words.","zh":"分析变化时，研究者可以采用实时分析，比较同一言语社区在两个不同时期的数据；如果变式的使用频率出现差异，就说明变化已经发生或正在发生。若缺少较早的数据，则可改用显时分析，比较年长者与年轻人的语言，其依据是个人语法在青春期后期趋于稳定。大约十八岁时形成的语言变项模式通常伴随我们一生，尽管我们仍会不断学到新词。"},{"en":"Sociolinguists also distinguish two kinds of change. Changes from above occur above the level of social awareness and typically involve adopting a prestigious or standardized variant from outside the community; the importation of r-fulness into New York City English is a classic case, as newer r-full forms gradually competed with and stigmatized the older r-less ones. Changes from below reflect articulatory or grammatical pressures within a system that speakers are generally unaware of, such as the Canadian English goose vowel moving toward the front of the vowel space.","zh":"社会语言学家还区分两类变化。来自上方的变化发生在社会意识层面之上，通常表现为从社区外部引入有威望或标准化的变式；带 r 的发音进入纽约市英语就是一个经典例子，较新的 r 音形式逐渐与较旧的无 r 形式竞争，并使后者日益受到污名化。来自下方的变化则反映语言系统内部发音或语法压力的作用，说话人通常毫无察觉，例如加拿大英语中 goose 的元音不断向元音空间前部移动。"},{"en":"Just as every language exhibits variation, every language also changes over time. Because change necessarily involves variation, variationist sociolinguists study changes in progress alongside stable variation.","zh":"正如每种语言都表现出变异，每种语言也都会随时间变化。由于变化必然涉及变异，变异社会语言学家既研究稳定变异，也研究进行中的变化。"}]},"chapter-119-slug-10-4-language-conveys-more-than-semantic-meaning":{"title":{"en":"10.4 Language conveys more than semantic meaning","zh":"10.4 语言传达的不只是语义"},"summary":{"en":"Language carries social meaning far beyond the semantic and pragmatic content of what is said. Forms of address, pronominal T/V distinctions, culturally variable interactional norms, contextual style and sociodemographic information all reveal social structure, power and identity, and speakers actively use variation to enact these meanings.","zh":"语言所承载的社会意义远超话语的语义和语用内容。称呼形式、代词性的 T/V 区分、受文化制约的互动规范、语境风格以及社会人口信息，都揭示出社会结构、权力关系与身份认同，而说话人也会主动运用变异来实施这些意义。"},"points":[{"en":"Forms of address reflect and enact social rank, gender perception, power dynamics and formality of the interaction.","zh":"称呼形式既反映也实施社会等级、性别认知、权力关系与互动正式程度。"},{"en":"Many Indo-European languages mark a T/V distinction, familiar versus formal second person singular, with real social consequences.","zh":"许多印欧语言区分亲昵与正式的第二人称单数 T/V，并带来现实的社会后果。"},{"en":"Taboo topics and conversational styles are culturally variable, so Euro-American norms must not be treated as universals.","zh":"禁忌话题与会话风格因文化而异，不能把欧美规范当作普遍标准。"},{"en":"Fischer (1958) showed standardized [ɪŋ] was used far more in formal than in casual contexts, demonstrating style shifting.","zh":"Fischer（1958）发现标准变式 [ɪŋ] 在正式语境中的使用远多于随意语境，从而展示了风格转换。"},{"en":"Labov's attention-paid-to-speech model links formality to self-monitoring; audience design and speaker design models add other motivations.","zh":"Labov 的注意力集中于言语模型把正式度与自我监控联系起来；受众设计模型与说话人设计模型补充了其他动因。"}],"terms":[{"en":"form of address","zh":"称呼形式","defEn":"A label used to refer to one's interlocutor, indexing rank and social circumstance.","defZh":"用来指称交谈对象的称谓，标示社会等级与互动情境。"},{"en":"T/V distinction","zh":"T/V 区分","defEn":"The contrast between familiar and formal second person singular pronouns, as in French tu and vous.","defZh":"第二人称单数代词亲昵形式与正式形式的对立，如法语的 tu 与 vous。"},{"en":"style shifting","zh":"风格转换","defEn":"Adjusting the frequency of variants according to the formality of the interactional context.","defZh":"依据互动语境的正式程度调整各变式的使用频率。"},{"en":"attention-paid-to-speech model","zh":"注意力集中于言语模型","defEn":"Labov's account linking formality and standardness to the degree of self-monitoring.","defZh":"Labov 的解释，把正式程度与标准化程度同自我监控的强度联系起来。"},{"en":"audience design model","zh":"受众设计模型","defEn":"Style shifting motivated by one's interlocutor or by people who might overhear.","defZh":"由交谈对象或可能偷听的人所引发的风格转换。"},{"en":"speaker design model","zh":"说话人设计模型","defEn":"Style shifting used to achieve interactional goals or highlight aspects of identity.","defZh":"为达成互动目标或凸显身份认同的某些方面而进行的风格转换。"}],"examples":[{"text":"sir, I know you’re upset but generally we don’t add steamed milk to iced coffees","noteEn":"The barista's initial polite form of address during the dispute.","noteZh":"咖啡师在争执初期使用的礼貌称呼形式。"},{"text":"listen buddy, it’s time for you to leave!","noteEn":"The shift from sir to buddy signals that politeness will no longer be maintained.","noteZh":"从 sir 转为 buddy，表明不再维持礼貌与正式的期待。"},{"text":"using tu instead of vous","noteEn":"A French speaker describes police address as part of degrading identity checks.","noteZh":"一位法语使用者把警察使用 tu 而非 vous 视为侮辱性盘查的一部分。"},{"text":"the boy used the standardized variant [ɪŋ] 97% of the time","noteEn":"Fischer's most formal context, the psychological test, from his 1958 study.","noteZh":"Fischer 1958 年研究中正式度最高的语境，即心理测试。"}],"body":[{"en":"Language reveals much about people, beyond the semantic and even pragmatic meaning of what is signed or spoken. Forms of address show how language both reflects and enacts social structure: in Canadian English, calling someone sir or buddy indexes perceived gender, the power dynamic between participants and the formality of the interaction. In a café dispute, a barista who moves from sir to buddy signals that the interactional context has changed and that the usual expectation of politeness has been abandoned.","zh":"语言透露出关于人的各种信息，其中许多超出了所说出或打出手势的语义乃至语用含义。称呼形式表明语言既反映又实施社会结构：在加拿大英语中，称对方为 sir 还是 buddy，透露了说话人对其性别的认知、双方之间的权力关系以及互动的正式程度。在咖啡馆的一场争执中，咖啡师从 sir 改称 buddy，表明互动语境已经改变，与服务工作相伴的礼貌期待也被放弃。"},{"en":"Many languages also encode social structure in pronominal reference. Numerous Indo-European languages distinguish a familiar, informal or lower-rank second person singular from a formal, polite or higher-rank one, the T/V distinction modeled on French tu and vous; Romance, Slavic and Germanic languages mark it. A French woman of Algerian immigrant roots described police who controlled her identity with tommy guns, racial insults and the use of tu instead of vous, placing that pronoun choice on the same level as symbolic and physical violence.","zh":"许多语言还把社会结构编码进代词指称之中。不少印欧语言区分亲昵、随意或低等级的第二人称单数形式与正式、礼貌或高等级的形式，即仿照法语 tu 与 vous 的 T/V 区分；罗曼语、斯拉夫语和日耳曼语都标示这一区分，古英语和中古英语亦然。一位阿尔及利亚移民背景的法国女性描述警察用冲锋枪查验身份、以种族侮辱相待并使用 tu 而非 vous，把这一代词选择与象征暴力和人身暴力相提并论。"},{"en":"Language also points to cultural values. What may be discussed, and with whom, is culturally determined, and conversational styles such as interactional overlap, tolerance for interruptions and eye-contact expectations vary culturally. Ball and Bernhardt (2008) observe that silence from a First Nations child, often read under Euro-American norms as ignorance or rudeness, may instead express respect for elders, so training a child by those norms could harm their connection with family culture.","zh":"语言还指向文化价值。可以谈论什么、与谁谈论都由文化决定，而互动重叠的程度、对打断的容忍度、目光接触的期待等会话风格同样随文化而变。Ball 与 Bernhardt（2008）指出，原住民儿童的沉默在欧美规范下常被解读为无知或无礼，但实际上可能是对长辈的尊重，因此按这些规范来训练儿童，可能会损害其与家庭文化的联系。"},{"en":"Contextual style is tied to the formality of interaction, which depends on the interlocutors' familiarity, their social similarity or difference and power relations, and the setting. Fischer's 1958 study of the -ing variable found one boy using standardized [ɪŋ] 97% of the time in a psychological test, 49% in a formal interview and 37% in an informal interview. Labov's attention-paid-to-speech model links formality and standardness to self-monitoring, while audience design and speaker design models explain shifts toward interlocutors or to identity goals.","zh":"语境风格与互动的正式程度密切相关，而正式程度取决于交谈双方的熟悉程度、社会相似性或差异与权力关系以及互动情境。Fischer 1958 年对 -ing 变项的研究发现，一名男孩在心理测试中使用标准变式 [ɪŋ] 的比例为 97%，在正式访谈中降至 49%，在非正式访谈中仅为 37%。Labov 的注意力集中于言语模型把正式程度与标准化程度同自我监控联系起来；受众设计模型与说话人设计模型则解释人们为何向交谈对象靠拢，或为达成互动与身份目标而转换风格。"}]},"chapter-120-slug-10-5-variationist-methods-and-concepts":{"title":{"en":"10.5 Variationist methods and concepts","zh":"10.5 变异社会语言学的方法与概念"},"summary":{"en":"Variationist sociolinguistics grew out of dialectology but replaced the N.O.R.M. participant with a wider social range and a focus on urban speech. Its hallmarks are the sociolinguistic interview, which uses tasks to vary self-monitoring and to approach the vernacular despite the observer's paradox, and the Principle of Accountability in quantitative analysis.","zh":"变异社会语言学脱胎于方言学，但不再局限于 N.O.R.M. 式发音人，而将社会范围大大拓宽并聚焦城市语言。其标志性方法是社会语言学访谈，通过不同任务调节自我监控程度并设法接近本真语体，同时应对观察者悖论；另一标志是量化分析中的问责原则。"},"points":[{"en":"Early dialectology preferred the N.O.R.M. participant, leaving urban, mobile, young and female speakers under-researched.","zh":"早期方言学偏好 N.O.R.M. 式发音人，使城市、流动、年轻及女性说话人长期缺乏研究。"},{"en":"Variationist sociolinguistics, sometimes called urban dialectology, widened the social factors considered beyond place.","zh":"变异社会语言学又称城市方言学，把考察的社会因素从地域扩展到更广的范围。"},{"en":"The sociolinguistic interview combines a Minimal Pairs task, a Reading Passage and Casual Speech to vary self-monitoring.","zh":"社会语言学访谈结合最小对立对任务、朗读短文与随意话语，以调节自我监控的程度。"},{"en":"The vernacular is unmonitored language, and the observer's paradox names the difficulty of recording it.","zh":"本真语体是未受监控的语言，而观察者悖论正是指出记录它的困难。"},{"en":"Many interviews from a representative, socially-stratified sample form a sociolinguistic corpus for quantitative study.","zh":"来自有代表性、社会分层的样本的多次访谈构成社会语言学语料库，供量化研究使用。"},{"en":"The Principle of Accountability requires counting a variant against all variants of the variable that could have occurred.","zh":"问责原则要求把某一变式与所有可能出现的变式相对照来统计。"}],"terms":[{"en":"N.O.R.M.","zh":"N.O.R.M.","defEn":"The early dialectologists' ideal participant: non-mobile, older, rural, male.","defZh":"早期方言学家理想中的发音人：不流动、年长、乡村、男性。"},{"en":"sociolinguistic interview","zh":"社会语言学访谈","defEn":"The main data-collection method, using tasks that vary the degree of self-monitoring.","defZh":"主要的数据收集方法，通过各类任务调节自我监控的程度。"},{"en":"vernacular","zh":"本真语体","defEn":"A speaker's unmonitored language, the way they talk when not being recorded.","defZh":"说话人未受监控的语言，即不被录音时的自然说话方式。"},{"en":"observer's paradox","zh":"观察者悖论","defEn":"We want speech free of systematic observation but can only obtain data by observing systematically.","defZh":"我们想要不受系统观察影响的言语，却只能通过系统观察来获取数据。"},{"en":"sociolinguistic corpus","zh":"社会语言学语料库","defEn":"A collection of sociolinguistic interviews used to study many variables and phenomena.","defZh":"由多次社会语言学访谈汇集的语料，用于研究多种变项与现象。"},{"en":"Principle of Accountability","zh":"问责原则","defEn":"Every variant must be counted against the total contexts in which any variant could occur.","defZh":"任何变式都必须与它可能出现的全部语境总数相对照进行统计。"}],"examples":[{"text":"giiwe [ɡiːweː] (‘he goes home’) and giiwenh [ɡiːwẽː] (‘so the story goes’)","noteEn":"A minimal pair in Southwestern Ojibwe differing only in final vowel nasalization.","noteZh":"明尼苏达西南部奥吉布瓦语中的最小对立对，仅以末元音是否鼻化相区别。"},{"text":"the word sore can be pronounced as [sɔɹ] or [sɔə]","noteEn":"In New York City English the r-less variant makes sore identical to saw.","noteZh":"在纽约市英语中，无 r 的变式使 sore 与 saw 发音相同。"},{"text":"nyuz ‘news’ and shuts ‘shoots’","noteEn":"Hawaiian Creole vowels vary continuously between [uː] and [ʉː] and in between.","noteZh":"夏威夷克里奥尔语的元音在 [uː] 与 [ʉː] 之间连续变化。"},{"text":"10/40 tokens of -ing = 25%","noteEn":"The accountable calculation for the reading passage in Table 10.1.","noteZh":"表 10.1 中朗读短文按问责原则得出的比例。"}],"body":[{"en":"Variationist methods descend from dialectology, the study of variation across regional varieties. Early dialectologists sought the N.O.R.M. participant, non-mobile, older, rural and male, assuming that non-mobile speakers preserve local speech, that older speakers represent older language, that rural areas retain tradition, and that women's speech is more status-conscious. This left gaps for urban variation, women's language and innovation, which variationist sociolinguistics, sometimes called urban dialectology, filled while adding social factors beyond place. Notable projects include the Atlas linguistique de la France and Yang Xiong's Fāngyán.","zh":"变异社会语言学的方法与方言学一脉相承，后者研究方言及地域变体之间的差异。早期方言学家追求 N.O.R.M. 式发音人，即不流动、年长、乡村、男性，其假设是：不流动者的言语更能代表原居地，年长者代表更古老的语言，乡村地区保留传统，而女性言语更在意身份地位。这导致城市变异、女性语言与创新用法研究出现空白，变异社会语言学（又称城市方言学）在填补空白的同时引入了地域之外的众多社会因素。重要的项目包括《法国语言地图集》和扬雄的《方言》。"},{"en":"The main data-collection method is the sociolinguistic interview, which originally combined a Minimal Pairs task, a Reading Passage and Casual Speech, designed to vary self-monitoring. In these minimal pairs one member contains a linguistic variable whose variant would make the two words identical, as with saw and sore in New York City English. Casual speech approaches the vernacular, a speaker's unmonitored language, yet recording it is complicated by the observer's paradox: we want to know how people talk when not systematically observed, but can obtain such data only through systematic observation.","zh":"最常用的数据收集方法是社会语言学访谈，它最初由最小对立对任务、朗读短文和随意话语组成，旨在调节自我监控的程度。这里使用的最小对立对中，有一个词含有语言变项，其某一变式会使两个词发音完全相同，如纽约市英语中的 saw 与 sore。随意话语接近本真语体，即说话人未受监控的语言；但记录它却受观察者悖论困扰：研究要弄清人们未被系统观察时如何说话，却只能通过系统观察获得这类数据。"},{"en":"A single interview shows how one person varies, but community patterning requires many interviews, ideally from a representative, socially-stratified sample, forming a sociolinguistic corpus. Important corpora include the Sankoff-Cedergren corpus of Montreal French, the first of its scale for a language other than English, and Sali Tagliamonte's Toronto English Archive.","zh":"一次访谈只能显示某一个人的变异方式，而要了解社区层面的模式则需要多次访谈，最好取自具有代表性、社会分层的样本，它们共同构成社会语言学语料库。重要的语料库包括蒙特利尔法语的 Sankoff-Cedergren 语料库，它是首个同等规模的非英语语料库，以及 Sali Tagliamonte 的多伦多英语档案。"},{"en":"Because variant choice is probabilistic, analysis must be quantitative and follow the Principle of Accountability: researchers count a variant against all variants that could have occurred, not just the interesting one. To study the [ɪn] variant of -ing, one needs the times [ɪn] occurred out of all the times it could have, so [ɪŋ] must also be counted. Table 10.1 shows why: counting only [ɪn] tokens makes the reading passage look higher than casual speech, 10 against 8, but adding the denominator reverses the picture, 25% against 40%.","zh":"由于变式的选择具有概率性，分析必须是量化的，并遵循问责原则：研究者要把某一变式与所有可能出现的变式相对照，而不能只统计自己感兴趣的那一个。例如要研究 -ing 的 [ɪn] 变式，就需要知道在它可能出现的情况下实际出现了多少次，因此也必须统计 [ɪŋ]。表 10.1 说明了这一点的重要性：只统计 [ɪn] 标记会让朗读短文看起来高于随意话语，即 10 对 8；但加入分母后结论正好相反，为 25% 对 40%。"}]},"chapter-121-slug-10-6-sociolinguistic-correlations-place":{"title":{"en":"10.6 Sociolinguistic correlations: Place","zh":"10.6 社会语言学相关性：地域"},"summary":{"en":"Place is the most obvious social dimension of variation: Egyptian and Syrian Arabic, or New York City and Chicago English, differ systematically. Standard varieties owe their prestige to power, as r-dropping shows, being admired in London but stigmatized in New York. Indexicality and enregisterment explain how features acquire regional meaning. Canadian English's unusual homogeneity reflects settler colonialism and the erasure of Indigenous languages, while eh is an enregistered Canadian marker whose actual frequency is low and regionally uneven.","zh":"地域是语言变异最直观的社会维度：埃及阿拉伯语与叙利亚阿拉伯语、纽约英语与芝加哥英语都有系统差异。标准变体的声望来自权力，非元音前 r 脱落即为例证，它在伦敦受推崇，在纽约却被贬低。指示性与语域化说明特征如何获得地域意义。加拿大英语异常同质，是定居者殖民主义与原住民语言被抹除的结果；而 eh 虽被语域化为加拿大标志，实际频率很低且因地区而异。"},"points":[{"en":"Regional varieties differ within and across nations, including Arabic, French, Spanish, English and Cree.","zh":"地域变体在国与国之间、一国内部都存在差异，阿拉伯语、法语、西班牙语、英语与克里语皆然。"},{"en":"A standard variety's prestige comes from power and politics, not from any linguistic superiority.","zh":"标准变体的声望源自权力与政治，而非语言本身的高下。"},{"en":"The same feature, non-prevocalic r deletion, indexes prestige in London but low status in New York.","zh":"同一特征即非元音前 r 脱落，在伦敦指向声望，在纽约却指向低社会地位。"},{"en":"Indexicality links features to social meaning through enregisterment, a process driven by cultural expectations.","zh":"指示性通过语域化把语言特征与社会意义相连，这一过程由文化预期驱动。"},{"en":"Canadian English spread homogeneously through settler colonialism, which erased Indigenous languages rather than fostering contact.","zh":"加拿大英语经由定居者殖民主义而同质地传播，它抹除原住民语言，而非促成语言接触。"}],"terms":[{"en":"indexicality","zh":"指示性","defEn":"The semiotic relation by which a linguistic feature points to some social or contextual meaning.","defZh":"语言特征指向某种社会或语境意义的符号关系，如地域、性别等含义。"},{"en":"enregisterment","zh":"语域化","defEn":"The process linking a particular linguistic feature with a cultural expectation, giving it social meaning.","defZh":"把某一语言特征与文化预期相联系、从而赋予其社会意义的过程。"},{"en":"non-prevocalic r deletion","zh":"非元音前 r 脱落（r-dropping）","defEn":"The variable absence of r after a vowel and before a consonant or pause, as in dark and car.","defZh":"元音后、辅音或停顿前的 r 可有可无地不发音，如 dark 与 car 中的情形。"},{"en":"pragmatic marker","zh":"语用标记","defEn":"A word or phrase, such as eh or you know, that manages interaction rather than adding content.","defZh":"如 eh、you know 等用于组织交际、而非增加命题内容的词或短语。"},{"en":"Principle of Accountability","zh":"责任原则","defEn":"The methodological requirement to count every variant of a variable, not only the variant of interest.","defZh":"要求统计一个变项的全部变式、而非只统计关注变式的方法论原则。"},{"en":"settler colonialism","zh":"定居者殖民主义","defEn":"Colonialism aimed at taking land so that settlers from the parent state can permanently repopulate it.","defZh":"以夺取土地、让宗主国定居者永久移居为目的的殖民主义类型。"}],"examples":[{"text":"dark [dɑːk] and car [cɑː]","noteEn":"Non-prevocalic r deletion in standard British English, perceived as prestigious in London.","noteZh":"标准英式英语中的非元音前 r 脱落，在伦敦被视为有声望的发音。"},{"text":"pretty cool","noteEn":"Among Canadian English speaking adolescents, the intensifier pretty tends to index masculinity.","noteZh":"在说加拿大英语的青少年中，强化副词 pretty 往往指向男性气质。"},{"text":"so cool","noteEn":"The intensifier so tends instead to index femininity among the same adolescents.","noteZh":"在同一群体中，强化副词 so 则往往指向女性气质。"},{"text":".@Canada’s now on Twitter, eh!","noteEn":"The Canadian government's first tweet, showing how thoroughly enregistered eh has become.","noteZh":"加拿大政府发布的第一条推文，显示 eh 已被高度语域化。"}],"body":[{"en":"When people imagine how the same language varies, region is usually the first thing that comes to mind. Dialectologists have long documented regional varieties: Egyptian and Syrian Arabic are distinct, as are Continental, Quebec and Moroccan French, and the Spanish of Mexico, Puerto Rico and Spain. Differences also occur inside a single country, with Acadian French opposed to Laurentian French, New York City English to Chicago English, and nēhiyawēwin (Plains Cree) to nīhithawīwin (Woods Cree). Sometimes one variety is elevated as the standard, but standardization always rests on power and politics rather than linguistic merit. Non-prevocalic r deletion, heard in dark [dɑːk] and car [cɑː], counts as prestigious Queen's English in London, yet the very same feature is judged non-standard and low-status in New York City.","zh":"提到同一种语言的不同说法，人们最先想到的往往是地域。方言学家早已记录下各地变体：埃及阿拉伯语与叙利亚阿拉伯语不同，大陆法语、魁北克法语与摩洛哥法语不同，墨西哥、波多黎各与西班牙的西班牙语也不同。差异同样存在于一国之内：阿卡迪亚法语与劳伦法语、纽约英语与芝加哥英语、nēhiyawēwin（平原克里语）与 nīhithawīwin（森林克里语）都相互区别。有时某一变体被抬升为标准语，但标准化从来与语言本身的性质无关，其根源始终是权力结构与政治。非元音前 r 脱落见于 dark [dɑːk] 与 car [cɑː]，在伦敦被视为有声望的女王英语，而同一特征在纽约却被判为非标准、低地位。"},{"en":"Regional variation also surfaces in vocabulary and in signed languages. A 2013 New York Times dialect quiz about soda versus pop asked readers which words they used and mapped their likely location in the United States; ASL likewise has several regional variants for BIRTHDAY, STRAWBERRY and PIZZA. Such associations arise through indexicality, the semiotic principle whereby a sign points to a meaning, here a place. Enregisterment links a feature with a cultural expectation, the same mechanism that makes pretty cool sound masculine and so cool feminine for Canadian adolescents.","zh":"地域差异也体现在词汇和手语中。2013 年《纽约时报》的方言测验围绕 soda 还是 pop 等用词提问，再据此推断读者在美国的大致位置；美国手语中 BIRTHDAY、STRAWBERRY、PIZZA 等词也有多个地域变体。这些联系经由指示性产生，即符号指向某种意义的符号学原理，此处指向的是地点。语域化把某一语言特征与文化预期连接起来，同样的机制使加拿大青少年口中的 pretty cool 显得男性化，而 so cool 显得女性化。"},{"en":"Canadian English is remarkably homogeneous from the Ontario-Quebec border to Vancouver Island, a fact usually traced to Loyalist settlers who migrated west. Contact with the many Indigenous languages of that territory left almost no trace, because settler colonialism sought land and the erasure of Indigenous peoples. The best-known enregistered Canadian feature is the pragmatic marker eh, celebrated on mugs and in the government's first tweet, yet accountability-based counts show it is rare: under one percent on Vancouver Island against 12% in Southern Ontario.","zh":"加拿大英语从安大略—魁北克边界到温哥华岛高度同质，通常被追溯至自美国迁来的效忠派定居者向西迁移。这一区域本有众多原住民语言，语言接触却几乎未留下痕迹，原因在于定居者殖民主义追求的是土地与原住民的消失。最著名的加拿大语域化特征是语用标记 eh，它被印在马克杯上，也成为加拿大政府的第一条推文；但按责任原则统计，eh 实际比例很低，温哥华岛不足百分之一，而安大略南部达 12%。"}]},"chapter-122-slug-10-7-sociolinguistic-correlations-social-status":{"title":{"en":"10.7 Sociolinguistic correlations: Social status","zh":"10.7 社会语言学相关性：社会地位"},"summary":{"en":"Social class is an intuitive but fuzzy concept, measured through occupation, education, income or residence. Labov's 1966 Lower East Side study showed that variants like the [ɪn] pronunciation of -ing are socially stratified, with working class speakers using it most. The same data reveal systematic style shifting, since every class uses more [ɪn] in casual speech than when reading. Variationist graphs should be read through caption, axes and legend before the author's description.","zh":"社会阶级是一个直观却模糊的概念，可用职业、教育、收入或居住地来衡量。拉博夫 1966 年对曼哈顿下东区的研究表明，-ing 读作 [ɪn] 等变式呈现社会分层，工人阶级使用最多。同一批数据还显示系统的语体转换：各个阶级在随意谈话中的 [ɪn] 都比朗读时多。读变异社会语言学的图表时，应先看标题说明、坐标轴与图例，再对照作者的描述。"},"points":[{"en":"Social class resists precise definition but can be diagnosed through occupation, education, income or residence.","zh":"社会阶级难以精确定义，但可用职业、教育、收入或居住地来判定。"},{"en":"Labov's 1966 Manhattan study classified speakers by occupation, education and income, finding class-linked variation.","zh":"拉博夫 1966 年的曼哈顿研究按职业、教育和收入划分说话人，发现变异与社会阶级相关。"},{"en":"The [ɪn] variant of -ing is stratified: working class speakers use it most, upper class speakers least.","zh":"-ing 的 [ɪn] 变式呈现分层：工人阶级使用最多，上层阶级最少。"},{"en":"Style shifting operates across all classes, since casual speech contains more [ɪn] tokens than reading passages.","zh":"语体转换在各阶级中普遍存在，随意谈话中的 [ɪn] 用例多于朗读段落。"},{"en":"Good graph reading starts with the caption, then the axes and legend, before the author's interpretation.","zh":"读图应先看标题说明，再看坐标轴和图例，最后才对照作者的解释。"}],"terms":[{"en":"social stratification","zh":"社会分层","defEn":"The systematic ranking of linguistic variants according to speakers' position in a social hierarchy.","defZh":"语言变式按说话人在社会等级中的位置而呈现的系统排序现象。"},{"en":"style shifting","zh":"语体转换","defEn":"A speaker's change in variant frequency as the formality of the context changes.","defZh":"说话人随语境正式程度变化而改变变式使用频率的现象。"},{"en":"linguistic variable","zh":"语言变项","defEn":"A linguistic item with two or more variants whose distribution is socially or contextually conditioned.","defZh":"具有两个或以上变式、其分布受社会或语境条件制约的语言项目。"},{"en":"variationist sociolinguistics","zh":"变异社会语言学","defEn":"The quantitative study of how linguistic variation correlates with social factors.","defZh":"定量研究语言变异与社会因素之间相关性的学科领域。"},{"en":"dependent variable","zh":"因变量","defEn":"In a graph, the thing measured, usually the proportion of one variant.","defZh":"图表中被测量的对象，通常指某一变式所占的比例。"},{"en":"independent variable","zh":"自变量","defEn":"The social or contextual factor that the researcher manipulated or controlled.","defZh":"研究者所操纵或控制的社会因素或语境因素。"}],"examples":[{"text":"[ɪn]","noteEn":"The non-standard variant of -ing whose frequency varies by social class.","noteZh":"非标准的 -ing 变式，其使用频率随社会阶级而变。"},{"text":"casual speech context contains more [ɪn] tokens than the reading passage context","noteEn":"Style shifting repeated across every social class in Labov's New York data.","noteZh":"拉博夫纽约数据中在每个社会阶级都重现的语体转换。"},{"text":"occupation, education, and income","noteEn":"Labov's three parameters for assigning speakers to social classes.","noteZh":"拉博夫为说话人划定社会阶级所用的三个参数。"}],"body":[{"en":"People generally have an intuition about social class and the unequal distribution of wealth and power it reflects, yet class is less tangible than age, gender or ethnicity. Since the Industrial Revolution, Euro-American societies have been divided into upper, middle and lower classes, a hierarchy mirrored in who holds wealth and power. Sociologists combine objective measures such as property, income and occupation with subjective ones such as life chances and prestige. In Canada, where most people count as middle class, those at the bottom of the hierarchy are marked by intersections with geography, race and ethnicity, with Black, Indigenous and other people of colour, especially recent immigrants, facing more precarious circumstances.","zh":"人们对社会阶级以及它所反映的财富与权力不平等通常有直觉，但阶级比年龄、性别或族裔更难把握。工业革命以来，欧美社会常被划分为上层、中层和下层三个群体，这一等级序列对应着财富与权力的分配。社会学定义既采用财产、收入、职业等客观指标，也采用生活机会、声望等主观指标。在加拿大，社会整体上以中产阶级为主，因此处于等级底端者更突出地体现在与地理、种族和族裔的交叠上：黑人、原住民及其他有色人种，尤其是新近移民，总体上处境更不稳定。"},{"en":"Because class is fuzzy, researchers need ways to diagnose it, often using occupation, education, income or residence. In his 1966 study of the Lower East Side of Manhattan, William Labov classified speakers using occupation, education and income, and found extensive correlations between variant frequencies and social class. The [ɪn] variant of -ing is socially stratified: working class speakers use it most, upper class speakers least, and the middle falls between. The same data show systematic style shifting, because in every social class casual speech contains more [ɪn] tokens than the reading passage context.","zh":"阶级概念虽模糊，却是直观的现实。要研究它作为语言变异条件的作用，就需要测量它的办法，常用职业、教育、收入或居住地来判断。在 1966 年对曼哈顿下东区英语的研究中，威廉·拉博夫用职业、教育和收入把说话人归入不同阶级，发现变式频率与个人社会阶级之间存在广泛相关。-ing 的 [ɪn] 变式呈现社会分层：工人阶级使用率最高，上层阶级最低，中间阶层介于两者之间。同一批数据还显示系统的语体转换：所有阶级在随意谈话中的 [ɪn] 用例都多于朗读段落。"},{"en":"Variationist sociolinguistics is quantitative, so graphs must be read carefully. A good caption names what was measured, the dependent variable, and what the researcher manipulated or controlled, the independent variables, usually in a by-statement, and identifies the source of the data. The axes and legend supply the details: the dependent variable normally sits on the vertical y-axis, while independent variables appear on the horizontal x-axis or as colours, shapes and line types. Finally, try to determine the pattern in the graph yourself before reading the author's description.","zh":"变异社会语言学是定量的，因此读图需要方法。好的标题说明会指明被测量的对象（因变量）以及研究者操纵或控制的因素（自变量），通常写作「因变量 by 自变量」，并注明数据来源。坐标轴和图例说明具体内容：因变量通常画在纵向的 y 轴，自变量画在横向的 x 轴，或用颜色、形状、线型在图例中区分。最后，应先自己判断图中的模式，再阅读作者的描述。"}]},"chapter-123-slug-10-8-sociolinguistic-correlations-gender":{"title":{"en":"10.8 Sociolinguistic correlations: Gender","zh":"10.8 社会语言学相关性：性别"},"summary":{"en":"Gender is a social acquisition, distinct from sex, which is itself partly constructed; early variationist work nevertheless collapsed the two. Labov codified gendered patterns as principles of linguistic change, with women favouring standard variants in stable variation and changes from above, yet leading changes from below. Gender ideologies, the double bind and covert prestige explain the first two principles, while Eckert's jocks and burnouts resolve the gender paradox through communities of practice. Trans and nonbinary linguists now examine nonbinary identity construction.","zh":"性别是社会习得的，与生理性别有别，而生理性别本身也部分是建构的；早期变异研究却常把二者混为一谈。拉博夫把性别化的变异模式总结为语言变化原则：稳定变异与自上而下的变化中女性更常用标准变式，而自下而上的变化中女性常是创新者。女性气质意识形态、双重束缚与隐性声望可解释前两条原则，埃克特的实践共同体研究则化解了性别悖论。跨性别与非二元语言学者的研究还考察非二元身份的建构。"},"points":[{"en":"Gender is socialized and distinct from sex, and even binary sex is partly a social construct.","zh":"性别经由社会化获得，与生理性别不同，而二元生理性别本身也部分是社会建构。"},{"en":"Early variationist studies correlated variables with perceived sex rather than gender, ignoring complexity.","zh":"早期变异研究把变项与研究者感知的生理性别而非性别相联系，忽视了性别的复杂性。"},{"en":"Labov's Principles I and Ia: women favour standard variants in stable variation and changes from above.","zh":"拉博夫的原则 I 与 Ia：在稳定变异和自上而下的变化中，女性更偏爱标准变式。"},{"en":"Principle II and the gender paradox: women lead changes from below, deviating from overt norms.","zh":"原则 II 与性别悖论：女性引领自下而上的变化，偏离显性规范。"},{"en":"Eckert's jocks and burnouts show that communities of practice, not gender alone, drive innovation.","zh":"埃克特研究的运动员与辍学群体表明，推动创新的是实践共同体而非性别本身。"},{"en":"Recent trans and nonbinary linguistics treats variation as a resource for constructing nonbinary identity.","zh":"近年跨性别与非二元语言学把变异视为建构非二元身份的资源。"}],"terms":[{"en":"gender","zh":"性别","defEn":"A social identity acquired through socialization, distinct from sex assigned at birth.","defZh":"经由社会化获得的社会身份，与出生时指派的生理性别不同。"},{"en":"double bind","zh":"双重束缚","defEn":"Lakoff's dilemma: women are criticized for tentative speech and for resisting that expectation.","defZh":"拉科夫提出的两难：女性既因犹疑无力的言语受批评，也因抗拒这一预期而被指责。"},{"en":"gender paradox","zh":"性别悖论","defEn":"Women conform more to overt norms than men, yet conform less when unobserved.","defZh":"女性比男性更遵从显性规范，却在无人注意时更少遵从。"},{"en":"covert prestige","zh":"隐性声望","defEn":"The positive value attached to non-standard, working class or racialized speech forms.","defZh":"非标准、工人阶级或族群化语言形式所获得的正面价值。"},{"en":"community of practice","zh":"实践共同体","defEn":"A group sharing interests, concerns and goals, whose members jointly negotiate linguistic norms.","defZh":"共享兴趣、关切与目标的群体，成员共同协商语言规范。"},{"en":"chain shift","zh":"链移","defEn":"A change in which several features move in a systematic, chronologically ordered serial way.","defZh":"若干特征按时间顺序系统性地相继移动的变化，如元音链移。"}],"examples":[{"text":"think [θɪŋk~tɪŋk] and with [ʍɪθ~ʍɪt]","noteEn":"Th-stopping in Detroit Black English, where /θ/ is realized as [θ] or [t].","noteZh":"底特律黑人英语中的 th 塞音化：/θ/ 实现为 [θ] 或 [t]。"},{"text":"“a girl is damned if she does, damned if she doesn’t.”","noteEn":"Lakoff's formulation of the double bind facing women's speech.","noteZh":"拉科夫对女性言语所面临双重束缚的概括。"},{"text":"the ‘jocks’","noteEn":"One of the two communities of practice at Eckert's Detroit-area high school.","noteZh":"埃克特所研究的高中的两个实践共同体之一：认同学校的运动员群体。"},{"text":"the ‘burnouts’","noteEn":"The anti-school, anti-authority community of practice in the same high school.","noteZh":"同一所高中里反学校、反权威的实践共同体。"}],"body":[{"en":"Gender is a social acquisition produced through lifelong socialization, while sex is assigned at birth on biological grounds. The common claim that gender is the social counterpart of biological sex is half true, because binary sex is itself a social construct: anatomical, endocrinal and chromosomal criteria form continua split at a socially drawn, fuzzy boundary. Cisgender, transgender, nonbinary and genderqueer people relate differently to their assigned sex. Many cultures, including two-spirit people in some Indigenous North American communities and hijras in India, recognize more than two genders.","zh":"性别是通过终身社会化获得的社会属性，而生理性别在出生时依据生物学指派。人们常说性别是生理性别的社会建构对应物，这只说对了一半：二元生理性别本身也是社会建构，解剖、内分泌和染色体标准都构成连续统，男女之间的界线是社会划定的、模糊的。顺性别者的性别认同大体与出生时指派的生理性别一致，跨性别者则与之不同，非二元与性别酷儿者的认同也不映射到男性气质与女性气质的谱系上。世界上不少文化并不限于二元性别，例如北美一些原住民社群中的双灵人（two-spirit）和印度的海吉拉（hijras）。"},{"en":"Gendered patterns of variation recur so consistently that Labov codified them as principles of linguistic change. In stable variation, women use the standardised variant more than men; in changes from above, women favour the incoming prestige variant; in changes from below, women are most often the innovators. Gender ideologies explain the first two: the girlie-girl trope ties prestige speech to canonical femininity, and standard language can protect the face of a relatively powerless speaker.","zh":"性别化的变异模式反复出现，拉博夫因此将其归纳为语言变化原则。在稳定变异中，女性比男性更常使用标准变式；在自上而下的变化中，女性更偏爱新进入的声望变式；在自下而上的变化中，女性往往是最主要的创新者。前两条可用性别意识形态解释：小公主式的刻板印象把有声望的语言与正统女性气质相连，而杜查尔（Deuchar）认为标准语能保护相对无权者的面子，在父权支配下成为一种生存策略。"},{"en":"Lakoff adds the double bind: women are socialized into tentative, powerless speech, yet are criticized for it, and risk being deemed unfeminine if they resist. Trudgill's Norwich study found most women over-reported their use of standard forms, but men are status-conscious too. Most men he interviewed believed they were more non-standard than they were, because non-prestigious working class and Black forms carry covert prestige, indexing the toughness and physical dominance attributed to those men.","zh":"拉科夫补充了双重束缚：女性被社会化为使用犹疑、无力的语言，却因此受批评，若抗拒这一预期又可能被指为不女性化。特鲁吉尔在诺里奇的研究发现，多数女性高报了自己使用标准变式的比例，但男性同样在意地位。他访谈的多数男性认为自己比实际更不标准，因为非声望的工人阶级语言与黑人语言具有隐性声望，指向阶级与种族意识形态赋予这些男性的强悍与体能优势。"},{"en":"The gender paradox observes that women conform more to overt norms yet less when unobserved, and it holds only in aggregate. Eckert's Detroit-area high school study contrasted school-oriented jocks with anti-authority burnouts, two communities of practice. With older changes of the Northern Cities Chain Shift, gender predicts variation best; with newer ones, community of practice matters most, and differences are larger for girls. Evaluated on symbolic capital, women maintain more rigid social boundaries. Trans and nonbinary linguists now show variation can perform a nonbinary identity.","zh":"性别悖论指出，女性更遵从显性规范，却在无人注意时更偏离规范，这一模式只在总体上成立。埃克特在底特律郊区高中的研究对比了认同学校的运动员群体与反权威的辍学群体，二者是不同的实践共同体。在北方城市链移中较旧的变化上，性别最能预测变异；在较新的变化上，实践共同体最重要，且女生两个群体间的差距更大。女性的地位取决于象征资本、取决于她看起来是谁，因此维持更严格的社会边界。近年跨性别与非二元语言学者则表明，变异可用于展演非二元身份。"}]},"chapter-124-slug-10-9-sociolinguistic-correlations-ethnicity":{"title":{"en":"10.9 Sociolinguistic correlations: Ethnicity","zh":"10.9 社会语言学的相关因素：族群"},"summary":{"en":"Ethnicity, like gender, is socioculturally complex, and language and ethnicity are often co-constitutive. Because people tend to interact mostly with co-ethnics, ethnically homogeneous social networks give rise to ethnolects. The case of Lumbee English shows how a unique mix of shared and unique features can document a community's long history of language use.","zh":"与性别一样，族群在社会文化与语言层面都很复杂，语言与族群常常互相界定、互为构成。由于人们大多与同族群成员往来，族群同质的社会网络催生了族群方言。北卡罗来纳州伦比人的英语正是例证：它由与邻近方言共享的特征和自身独有的特征混合而成，可用来证明该社群长期使用英语的历史。"},"points":[{"en":"Language and ethnicity are mutually defining, so the boundaries of languages often mirror ethnic group boundaries.","zh":"语言与族群互相界定，因此语言之间的分界往往映照出族群之间的分界。"},{"en":"People usually live near co-ethnics, producing ethnically homogeneous social networks and, in turn, ethnolects.","zh":"人们通常与同族群的人比邻而居，形成族群同质的社会网络，进而产生族群方言。"},{"en":"The Lumbee Tribe of North Carolina is the largest Indigenous group east of the Mississippi River.","zh":"北卡罗来纳州的伦比部落是密西西比河以东规模最大的原住民群体。"},{"en":"Lumbee English mixes features shared with neighbours with features unique to the community, notably perfective be.","zh":"伦比英语既包含与邻近群体共享的特征，也包含本社群独有的特征，其中最突出的是完成体 be。"},{"en":"Consonant cluster reduction and was/were levelling both show conditioning patterns unique to Lumbee English.","zh":"辅音丛简化和 was/were 拉平在伦比英语中都呈现出独有的制约模式。"}],"terms":[{"en":"ethnolect","zh":"族群方言","defEn":"A variety of an ambient standardized community language used by a minoritized ethnic group.","defZh":"被少数化的族群所使用的、当地主流（标准化）社区语言的一种变体。"},{"en":"perfective be","zh":"完成体 be","defEn":"Use of be rather than have as the auxiliary marking perfect aspect with certain main verbs.","defZh":"某些主动词用 be 而非 have 作助动词来标记完成体。"},{"en":"consonant cluster reduction","zh":"辅音丛简化","defEn":"Variation between complex codas and reduced codas, as in disk realized as [dɪsk] or [dɪs].","defZh":"复杂韵尾与简化韵尾之间的变项，如 disk 可读作 [dɪsk] 或 [dɪs]。"},{"en":"was/were levelling","zh":"was/were 拉平","defEn":"The variable realization of were as was, as in we were/was and they weren't/wasn't.","defZh":"were 可变地实现为 was，如 we were/was 与 they weren't/wasn't。"},{"en":"complementizer","zh":"标句词","defEn":"An element introducing an embedded clause, here the 'for to' complementizer of Lumbee English.","defZh":"引导嵌入小句的成分，此处指伦比英语的 for to 标句词。"},{"en":"federal recognition","zh":"联邦承认","defEn":"Formal tribal status granted by the US Government, for which the Lumbee have long petitioned.","defZh":"美国联邦政府授予的正式部落地位，伦比人长期为此请愿。"}],"examples":[{"text":"I want for to get it","noteEn":"Lumbee English 'for to' complementizer, shared only with Appalachian English among nearby varieties.","noteZh":"伦比英语的 for to 标句词，在附近变体中只与阿巴拉契亚英语共享。"},{"text":"she bes there","noteEn":"Finite be, a feature Lumbee English shares only with its Anglo-American neighbours.","noteZh":"限定性 be，这一特征在伦比英语中只与邻近的英裔美国人共享。"},{"text":"If I ’m got a dollar, I ’m got it.","noteEn":"Present-tense perfective be, which corresponds to standard English [have got] in this example.","noteZh":"现在时完成体 be，对应标准英语的 [have got]。"},{"text":"Sie hat ihren hund mitgebracht","noteEn":"German still alternates have and be in the perfect, as Lumbee English does.","noteZh":"德语在完成体中仍交替使用 have 与 be，与伦比英语相似。"}],"body":[{"en":"Ethnicity, like gender, is socioculturally complex, and language and ethnicity are often co-constitutive: the line between two languages is drawn with reference to the groups who speak them, while a group is often identified by the language it uses. Czech and Slovak are mutually intelligible yet treated as distinct languages. People tend to live near co-ethnics, so social networks are often homogeneous, and since speakers use language much as their most frequent interlocutors do, ethnolects emerge: varieties of an ambient standardized language used by a minoritized ethnic group. In North America that standard is ideologically tied to whiteness and European settlers.","zh":"与性别一样，族群在社会文化层面十分复杂，语言与族群常常互为构成：两种语言的分界是参照使用它们的群体来划定的，而一个群体又常常凭借其使用的语言来界定。捷克语与斯洛伐克语可以互相听懂，却被视为不同族群的不同语言。人们往往与同族群的人住得很近，因此社会网络常常是同质的；又由于说话人使用语言的方式与交往最频繁的人相似，族群方言便应运而生：它是被少数化的族群所使用的、当地主流标准化语言的一种变体。在北美洲，这种标准在意识形态上与白人性及欧洲移民相关联。"},{"en":"The Lumbee Tribe of North Carolina, with 45 000 members, is the largest Indigenous group east of the Mississippi River. Most Lumbee live in Robeson County, a multiethnic area that is 40% Lumbee, 35% Anglo-American and 25% African American. The Lumbees have state-recognized status but lack federal recognition, and their petitions have failed for nearly a century and a half. A key obstacle is language history: knowledge of an ancestral language is evidence of descent, but the Lumbee were documented speaking English as early as 1730. Sociolinguists therefore document the uniqueness and time depth of Lumbee English.","zh":"北卡罗来纳州的伦比部落有 45 000 名成员，是密西西比河以东最大的原住民群体。大多数伦比人住在罗伯逊县，这是一个多族群地区：40% 为伦比人，35% 为英裔美国人，25% 为非洲裔美国人。伦比人拥有北卡罗来纳州承认的地位，却未获联邦承认，其请愿在近一个半世纪里屡屡失败。一大障碍是语言史：掌握祖先语言是证明世系的关键证据，但有文献记载，伦比人早在 1730 年就已使用英语。于是社会语言学家着手记录伦比英语的独特性与时间深度。"},{"en":"Many features of Lumbee English are shared with neighbouring varieties, yet the combination is unique: /aɪ/ is raised and backed to [ɑ̝ɪ]; the 'for to' complementizer is shared only with Appalachian English; finite be only with Anglo-American neighbours. Perfective be is distinctive, for standard English once alternated be and have in the perfect depending on the main verb, as German still does. Lumbee English retains it in present and past tenses, and past-tense perfective be is unique to the community, suggesting very long use of English there. Consonant cluster reduction, as in disk realized [dɪsk] or [dɪs], and was/were levelling are shared variables, but Lumbee English conditions both in its own way.","zh":"伦比英语的许多特征与邻近方言共享，但这些特征的组合却是独一无二的：/aɪ/ 被抬高并后移为 [ɑ̝ɪ]；for to 标句词只与阿巴拉契亚英语共享；限定性 be 只与英裔美国邻居共享。完成体 be 独具特色：标准英语一度依主动词不同而在完成体中交替使用 be 与 have，德语至今如此；伦比英语在现在时和过去时中都保留了它，而过去时的完成体 be 是该社群独有的，说明当地使用英语的历史非常悠久。辅音丛简化（如 disk 读作 [dɪsk] 或 [dɪs]）与 was/were 拉平虽为共享变项，但伦比英语对二者的制约方式自成一套。"}]},"chapter-125-slug-10-10-three-waves-of-sociolinguistics":{"title":{"en":"10.10 Three waves of sociolinguistics","zh":"10.10 社会语言学的三次浪潮"},"summary":{"en":"Eckert (2012) distinguishes three waves in variationist sociolinguistics: correlating variation with macro-social facts, attending to locally relevant social categories, and emphasising speakers' agency and the social meaning of variation. Kiesling's study of fraternity men illustrates the third wave, showing how the non-standard [ɪn] variant expresses stances and reshapes the context.","zh":"埃克特（2012）把变异社会语言学划分为三次浪潮：将变异与宏观社会事实相关联、关注地方性相关的社会范畴，以及强调说话人的能动性与变异的社会意义。基斯林对大学兄弟会成员的研究体现了第三次浪潮，说明非标准变体 [ɪn] 如何表达立场并重塑语境。"},"points":[{"en":"First-wave variationists correlated linguistic variation with macro-sociodemographic facts about participants.","zh":"第一次浪潮的变异研究者把语言变异与受试者的宏观社会人口特征相关联。"},{"en":"Second-wave work showed locally relevant facts, such as high school cliques, can predict linguistic behaviour better than class.","zh":"第二次浪潮表明，高中小团体之类地方性相关事实比社会阶级更能预测语言行为。"},{"en":"The third wave foregrounds speakers' agency and the social meanings constructed through variation.","zh":"第三次浪潮突出说话人的能动性以及通过变异建构的社会意义。"},{"en":"Kiesling found three fraternity men used more non-standard [ɪn] in formal meetings, expressing stances and reframing the event.","zh":"基斯林发现三名兄弟会成员在正式会议上更多使用非标准 [ɪn]，以表达立场并重塑活动氛围。"},{"en":"Non-standard speech indexes working class toughness, which in turn indexes dominance or, alternatively, solidarity.","zh":"非标准言语指涉工人阶级的强硬，进而指涉支配地位，或者反过来指涉团结一致。"}],"terms":[{"en":"variationist sociolinguistics","zh":"变异社会语言学","defEn":"The subfield studying the link between language and society through patterns of linguistic variation.","defZh":"通过语言变异的模式来研究语言与社会之间关系的分支学科。"},{"en":"first wave","zh":"第一次浪潮","defEn":"Work correlating variation with macro-sociodemographic facts and pre-existing social stratification.","defZh":"把变异与宏观社会人口事实及既有的社会分层相关联的研究。"},{"en":"second wave","zh":"第二次浪潮","defEn":"Work recognising that locally relevant facts about speakers also shape linguistic variation.","defZh":"承认说话人的地方性相关事实同样塑造语言变异的研究。"},{"en":"third wave","zh":"第三次浪潮","defEn":"The current paradigm emphasising speakers' agency and the social meaning of variation.","defZh":"当前范式，强调说话人的能动性以及变异所承载的社会意义。"},{"en":"variable -ing","zh":"-ing 变项","defEn":"The alternation between standard [ɪŋ] and non-standard [ɪn] in words like socializing or meetings.","defZh":"socializing、meetings 等词中标准式 [ɪŋ] 与非标准式 [ɪn] 之间的交替。"},{"en":"covert prestige","zh":"隐性声望","defEn":"The positive value attached to non-standard variants within a community, discussed in section 10.8.","defZh":"社群内部赋予非标准变体的正面价值，第 10.8 节曾讨论。"}],"examples":[{"text":"You guys know I’m a fuckin’ hard worker. I work my ass off for everything.","noteEn":"A fraternity member pitching for election while expressing a working class persona.","noteZh":"一名兄弟会成员在竞选演说中表达工人阶级形象。"},{"text":"And I’m not gonna pay this fuckin’ money. All right?","noteEn":"The fraternity president uses non-standard forms to express a confrontational stance.","noteZh":"兄弟会主席使用非标准形式来表达对抗性的立场。"},{"text":"Maisie uses a high rate of the standardized [ɪŋ] variant because she is a young, white, middle class, woman.","noteEn":"A first-wave interpretation that links variation directly to macro-social categories.","noteZh":"第一次浪潮式的解读，把变异直接关联到宏观社会范畴。"}],"body":[{"en":"Recent variationist work does not merely explain variation by social factors; it asks how variation expresses and constructs socially meaningful distinctions. Penelope Eckert (2012) identified three waves. First-wave variationists correlated variation with macro-sociodemographic facts, treating patterns as products of existing stratification. Second-wave researchers recognised that locally relevant facts matter just as much: a high school student's clique may predict linguistic behaviour better than social class. In the current third wave the paradigm has shifted to speakers' agency and the social meaning of variation, asking how individuals use variation to construct their position in society and how variables combine into styles signalling many social meanings.","zh":"晚近的变异研究不再仅仅用社会因素解释变异，而是追问变异如何表达并建构具有社会意义的分野。佩内洛普·埃克特（2012）指出该领域有三次浪潮。第一次浪潮的变异研究者把变异与宏观社会人口事实相关联，视变异模式为既有社会分层的产物。第二次浪潮认识到，地方性相关的事实同样重要：高中生所属的小团体或许比社会阶级更能预测其语言行为。当前的第三次浪潮则转向说话人的能动性与变异的社会意义，追问个人如何用变异建构社会位置，以及变项如何组合成传达多种社会意义的风格。"},{"en":"Scott Kiesling's (1998) study of fraternity men exemplifies the third wave. He recorded members of an American university fraternity while socialising and during formal meetings run by parliamentary procedure. The men used far more non-standard [ɪn] when socialising, but three members reversed this pattern and used more [ɪn] in the formal context. Kiesling found the variant carrying stance and identity. One member campaigning for the executive council presented himself as a hard-working, working class man who tells it like it is; his [ɪn] expressed that persona and reframed the meeting as camaraderie. The fraternity president used more [ɪn] in meetings to express a confrontational stance.","zh":"斯科特·基斯林（1998）对兄弟会成员的研究是第三次浪潮的范例。他记录了美国一所大学兄弟会成员在社交场合和由议事程序主导的正式会议上的言语。这些人在社交时使用非标准 [ɪn] 远多于会议，但有三名成员相反，在正式场合更多使用 [ɪn]。基斯林发现该变体承载着立场与身份。一名竞选执行委员会职位的成员把自己呈现为勤奋、直言的工人阶级男性，他用 [ɪn] 表达这种形象，并把会议气氛重塑为同袍情谊。兄弟会主席则用更多的 [ɪn] 表达对抗性立场。"},{"en":"Kiesling links these uses to the cultural discourse of dominance: to be a man is to be strong and authoritative. Dominance is readily expressed through real authority and power, so working class men, who often lack these, must express it through physical means. Men draw on this association when using non-standard variants, as covert prestige suggests: non-standard speech indexes working class toughness, which indexes dominance. The president illustrates this, while the campaigning speaker signalled solidarity. Maisie uses a high rate of [ɪŋ] because she is young, white, middle class and a woman (first wave), because she is a 'jock' (second wave), or because she constructs an educated identity (third wave).","zh":"基斯林把这些用法与关于支配的文化话语联系起来：做男人就要坚强而有权威。支配最容易通过真实的权威与权力表达，因此往往缺乏这些的工人阶级男性只能借助身体手段来表现。男性在使用非标准变体时都调用了这种联想，正如隐性声望所示：非标准言语指涉工人阶级的身体强硬，进而指涉支配。主席正是如此，而那位竞选者表达的是团结。三次浪潮对同一份数据的解读也不同：梅茜高频使用 [ɪŋ]，第一次浪潮解释为她年轻、白人、中产阶级且是女性，第二次浪潮解释为她属于“运动型”群体，第三次浪潮则解释为她借此建构受过教育的身份。"}]},"chapter-126-slug-10-11-summary":{"title":{"en":"10.11 Summary","zh":"10.11 小结"},"summary":{"en":"Variationist sociolinguistics examines linguistic variables, the multiple ways of doing the same thing, to link language and society. It has revealed facts about linguistic structure, social structure and language change, and it differs from dialectology in its data, methods and social range. Early work sought correlations with social factors; modern work focuses on speakers' agentive use of variation for identity work.","zh":"变异社会语言学研究语言变项，即做同一件事的多种方式，以此连接语言与社会。它揭示了关于语言结构、社会结构与语言变化的事实，并在数据、方法与社会范围上区别于方言学。早期研究寻求变异与社会因素的相关，现代研究则关注说话人能动地运用变异进行身份建构。"},"points":[{"en":"Linguistic variables, two or more ways of doing the same thing, are the primary object of study.","zh":"语言变项，即做同一件事的两种或更多方式，是主要研究对象。"},{"en":"Variables occur in all varieties and all domains of language, from phonetics to pragmatics.","zh":"变项存在于一切语言变体和一切语言领域之中，从语音到语用。"},{"en":"The field diverged from dialectology in data, quantitative methods and its full social range of speakers.","zh":"该领域在数据、定量方法以及覆盖全部社会范围的说话人方面与方言学分道扬镳。"},{"en":"This chapter treated style, place, social status, gender and ethnicity, though many more factors matter.","zh":"本章讨论了风格、地点、社会地位、性别与族群，但重要因素远不止这些。"},{"en":"Modern approaches ask how people use variation for identity work, aligning with or distancing from others.","zh":"现代研究路径追问人们如何用变异做身份建构，与他人结盟或疏远。"}],"terms":[{"en":"linguistic variable","zh":"语言变项","defEn":"Two or more ways of doing the same thing, the primary object of variationist study.","defZh":"做同一件事的两种或更多方式，是变异研究的主要对象。"},{"en":"variant","zh":"变式","defEn":"One of the alternative realizations that a linguistic variable may take.","defZh":"某一语言变项在实际言语中可能采取的几种交替实现形式之一。"},{"en":"sociolinguistic interview","zh":"社会语言学访谈","defEn":"A recording method characteristic of variationist fieldwork data collection.","defZh":"变异社会语言学田野调查中用于收集自然口语数据的代表性录音方法。"},{"en":"NORMS","zh":"NORMS（非流动年长男性乡村说话人）","defEn":"Non-mobile older rural males, the traditional dialectological informants the field moved beyond.","defZh":"非流动的年长乡村男性，即该领域已超越的传统方言学调查对象。"}],"examples":[],"body":[{"en":"Variationist sociolinguistics investigates the link between language and society by examining linguistic variation. Its primary object is the linguistic variable: two or more ways of doing the same thing. Variables are found in every variety and every domain of language, from phonetics to pragmatics. By studying the patterns between the variants of a variable and their sociocultural context, the field has uncovered facts about linguistic structure, social structure and the process of language change. It has roots in dialectology but became distinct in its data (sociolinguistic interviews and corpora), its analytic methods (quantitative and statistical techniques) and its focus, which extends to language in use across the whole social range of a speech community rather than only NORMS.","zh":"变异社会语言学通过考察语言变异来研究语言与社会之间的联系。它的主要对象是语言变项，即做同一件事的两种或更多方式。变项存在于一切语言变体和一切语言领域之中，从语音到语用。通过研究变项各变式与其社会文化语境之间的模式，该领域揭示了关于语言结构、社会结构以及语言变化过程的事实。它源于方言学，但在数据（社会语言学访谈与语料库）、分析方法（定量与统计技术）以及关注点上都已独立：它扩展到对整个言语社群全部社会范围内使用中的语言的研究，而不局限于 NORMS。"},{"en":"This chapter considered several sociocultural factors: style, place, social status, gender and ethnicity. Many further factors have also proved important for understanding variation. Early variationist research tried to explain variation by finding correlations between social factors and patterns of variation. Modern approaches instead focus on how people agentively use language and linguistic variation for identity work, to align with or disalign from others, and to shape and reshape the social context.","zh":"本章讨论了几种社会文化因素：风格、地点、社会地位、性别与族群。此外还有许多因素被证明对理解变异十分重要。早期的变异研究试图通过寻找社会因素与变异模式之间的相关来解释变异。现代路径则转而关注人们如何能动地运用语言和语言变异来进行身份建构，与他人结盟或疏远，并塑造和重塑社会语境。"}]},"chapter-127-slug-10-12-exercise-your-linguistic-skills":{"title":{"en":"10.12 Exercise Your Linguistics Skills","zh":"10.12 锻炼你的语言学技能"},"summary":{"en":"This closing exercise set asks readers to apply the chapter's variationist tools to four problems: identifying variables in New York City English minimal pairs, cataloguing quotative verbs in reported speech, weighing Labov's distinction between markers and indicators, and evaluating a bar plot of hypothetical Auslan data on gender and sign variants.","zh":"本组收尾练习要求读者把本章的变异研究工具用于四个具体问题：辨识纽约市英语最小对立对中的语言变项，搜集报告言语里的引语动词，评估拉博夫关于标记项与指示项的区分，并检验一幅关于澳大利亚手语性别差异数据的柱状图。"},"points":[{"en":"Identify the linguistic variable and its variants in the New York City English pairs saw~sore, udder~other, beg~bag, and bruin~brewing.","zh":"指出纽约市英语 saw~sore、udder~other、beg~bag、bruin~brewing 各对词中的语言变项及其变式。"},{"en":"Reported speech is introduced by quotative verbs; the task is to collect their variants across the chapter and propose additional ones.","zh":"报告言语由引语动词引出，需搜集本章出现的各种变式，并补充其他可能的说法。"},{"en":"Labov distinguishes markers, which correlate with both social class and style, from indicators, which correlate only with social class.","zh":"拉博夫区分标记项与指示项：前者同时与社会阶层和语体相关，后者只与社会阶层相关。"},{"en":"In the hypothetical Auslan data, readers evaluate the bar plot, redraw it, then judge which gender group favours or avoids the lowered variant.","zh":"针对假设的澳大利亚手语数据，读者需评价原有柱状图、重新作图，并判断哪一性别群体最倾向或最少使用降低变式。"}],"terms":[{"en":"minimal pair","zh":"最小对立对","defEn":"Two words that differ in one sound and signal different meanings.","defZh":"只在某一个语音上存在差别、却表示不同意义的两个词，可用于确定音位。"},{"en":"variable","zh":"变项","defEn":"A linguistic feature that varies across speakers or contexts; its alternate forms are variants.","defZh":"在不同说话人或不同语境中发生变化的语言特征，其不同表现形式称为变式。"},{"en":"marker","zh":"标记项","defEn":"A variable that correlates with both social class and contextual style.","defZh":"同时与社会阶层和语境语体相关的语言变项，其变化反映说话人对言语的注意程度。"},{"en":"indicator","zh":"指示项","defEn":"A variable that correlates with social class but not with contextual style.","defZh":"只与社会阶层相关，而不随语境语体变化的语言变项，拉博夫称之为指示项。"},{"en":"reported speech","zh":"报告言语（引述言语）","defEn":"A representation of some past or hypothetical speech or thought.","defZh":"对过去或假设的言语、思想的转述，英语书面上通常置于引号之内。"},{"en":"quotative verb","zh":"引语动词","defEn":"A verb such as to say that introduces reported speech.","defZh":"用来引出报告言语的动词，英语中常见的是 to say。"}],"examples":[{"text":"saw~sore","noteEn":"A New York City English minimal pair whose second word contains a linguistic variable.","noteZh":"纽约市英语的一对最小对立对，其中第二个词包含一个语言变项。"},{"text":"udder~other","noteEn":"Another listed minimal pair, used to work out a variable and its variants.","noteZh":"文中列出的另一对最小对立对，用于推断变项及其变式。"},{"text":"beg~bag","noteEn":"A minimal pair from section 10.5 that readers are asked to analyse.","noteZh":"10.5 节给出的一对最小对立对，读者需对其加以分析。"},{"text":"bruin~brewing","noteEn":"The fourth minimal pair, whose second member again contains a different variable.","noteZh":"第四对最小对立对，其第二个词同样包含另一个不同的变项。"}],"body":[{"en":"The final exercise set asks readers to apply the chapter's tools for variation to fresh problems. Exercise 1 goes back to the New York City English minimal pairs saw~sore, udder~other, beg~bag, and bruin~brewing, all of which were listed in section 10.5. Readers must work out which linguistic variable each pair illustrates, propose its variants, and then ask whether the same pairs would count as minimal pairs in their own variety of English.","zh":"本组练习要求读者把本章的变异研究工具用于新的问题。练习 1 回到 10.5 节列举的纽约市英语最小对立对 saw~sore、udder~other、beg~bag 和 bruin~brewing，要求读者判断每对词体现的是哪一个语言变项、提出其变式，并思考这些词对在自己熟悉的英语变体中是否同样构成最小对立对。"},{"en":"Exercise 2 turns to reported speech, that is, representations of past or hypothetical speech and thought. English usually introduces such material with a quotative verb like to say, and writes it inside quotation marks. Readers skim the chapter for every token of reported speech, list the quotative verbs that introduce them, count the variants, and suggest others.","zh":"练习 2 关注报告言语，即对过去或假设的言语与思想的呈现。英语通常用 to say 一类引语动词引出这类内容，并在书面上加上引号。读者需要回看本章，找出所有报告言语用例，记录引出它们的引语动词，统计出现了多少种变式，并再想一些其他变式。"},{"en":"Exercise 3 raises a theoretical point from Labov. Not every variable that correlates with social class also correlates with contextual style. Variables of the first kind, called markers, pattern with both social class and style; indicators, by contrast, correlate with social class alone. The exercise asks why this distinction matters, hinting that style-shifting is itself a matter of how much attention speakers pay to their speech.","zh":"练习 3 涉及拉博夫提出的一个理论区分。并非所有与社会阶层相关的变项都会随语境语体变化：同时与社会阶层和语体相关的变项叫标记项，只与社会阶层相关的变项叫指示项。练习要求读者思考这一区分的重要性，并提示语体差异实际上也反映了说话人对自身言语的注意程度。"},{"en":"Exercise 4 supplies hypothetical Auslan data on the citation form and lowered variant of the signs KNOW, THINK, and NAME, collected from sociolinguistic interviews with men, women, and nonbinary signers. Table 10.2 gives the token counts, and Figure 10.8 is the colleague's bar plot. Readers must explain, using the chapter's concepts, why the plot represents the data poorly, draw a better graph, and state which group is most and which least likely to use the lowered variant.","zh":"练习 4 给出一组关于澳大利亚手语（Auslan）的假设数据，涉及 KNOW、THINK、NAME 三个手语符号的引用形式与降低变式，语料来自对自我认同为男性、女性和非二元性别的谈话人所作的社会语言学访谈。表 10.2 列出各形式的用例数，图 10.8 是同事据此绘制的柱状图。读者需运用本章概念，说明该图为何不是呈现这些数据的最佳方式，另绘一幅更合适的图，并指出哪一群体最可能、哪一群体最不可能使用降低变式。"}]}});
